{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013WX17JPvqZKpApWhhuKzzH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\ttier: best\n2\tnarrative_guidance: 'Original Redundancy Paradox framing holds completely. Three claims\n3\t  require no softening. Story: CKA-aware pruning preserves reasoning scaffolding →\n4\t  RLVR recovery redistributes function across retained structures → 65-78% recovery\n5\t  vs <18% for standard pruning. GRASPrune as intermediate baseline confirms CKA is\n6\t  the missing ingredient. Mixed-effects R²>0.25 validates CKA overlap as a per-structure\n7\t  recoverability predictor. RLVR vs SFT interaction supports \"RL Squeezes, SFT Expands\"\n8\t  mechanism. Clean EMNLP main paper with all three claims as section-level contributions.\n9\t\n10\t  '\n11\timagined_experiments:\n12\t- name: Full GSM8K Evaluation (Baseline + RASP_20 + Standard_20)\n13\t  plan_id: plan_001\n14\t  description: 'Full 1319-sample GSM8K test evaluation for three configs: unpruned\n15\t    baseline, RASP at 20% sparsity, and Standard (importance-only) at 20% sparsity.\n16\t    Replaces N=100 quick evals with publication-quality numbers and 95% CIs.\n17\t\n18\t    '\n19\t  expected_results:\n20\t    baseline_gsm8k_accuracy: 0.832\n21\t    baseline_gsm8k_ci95:\n22\t    - 0.811\n23\t    - 0.852\n24\t    baseline_n_correct: 1097\n25\t    rasp_20_gsm8k_accuracy: 0.289\n26\t    rasp_20_gsm8k_ci95:\n27\t    - 0.265\n28\t    - 0.314\n29\t    rasp_20_n_correct: 381\n30\t    standard_20_gsm8k_accuracy: 0.014\n31\t    standard_20_gsm8k_ci95:\n32\t    - 0.007\n33\t    - 0.024\n34\t    standard_20_n_correct: 18\n35\t    rasp_vs_standard_gap_pp: 27.5\n36\t    rasp_vs_standard_mcnemar_p: <0.0001\n37\t    rasp_retention_rate: 0.347\n38\t    standard_retention_rate: 0.017\n39\t  result_rationale: 'Baseline settles at ~83% on full GSM8K (quick eval''s 94/100\n40\t    was upward-biased by small N and favorable sample draw; 1319-sample eval regresses\n41\t    toward the known DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B population accuracy of ~82-84% on\n42\t    GSM8K). RASP_20 at 28.9% is consistent with quick eval''s 29/100, confirming that\n43\t    CKA-aware structure selection preserves enough reasoning scaffolding for ~35%\n44\t    retention. The key mechanism: RASP retains functionally unique heads (low F(h))\n45\t    that encode irreplaceable reasoning steps—removing high-overlap heads leaves structures\n46\t    whose function can potentially be redistributed during recovery. Standard_20 at\n47\t    1.4% confirms importance-only pruning collapses reasoning: it preferentially retains\n48\t    high-overlap \"safe\" structures while removing functionally unique ones, destroying\n49\t    the reasoning scaffold entirely. The 27.5pp gap widens slightly from quick eval''s\n50\t    28pp due to full-set evaluation capturing more multi-step problems where reasoning\n51\t    chain integrity matters. McNemar''s test is trivially significant at N=1319.\n52\t\n53\t    '\n54\t  tables:\n55\t  - name: 'Table 1: Post-Prune GSM8K Accuracy (1319 samples)'\n56\t    columns:\n57\t    - Method\n58\t    - Sparsity\n59\t    - GSM8K Acc (%)\n60\t    - 95% CI\n61\t    - Retention (%)\n62\t    rows:\n63\t    - - Baseline\n64\t      - 0%\n65\t      - 83.2\n66\t      - '[81.1, 85.2]'\n67\t      - —\n68\t    - - RASP\n69\t      - 20%\n70\t      - 28.9\n71\t      - '[26.5, 31.4]'\n72\t      - 34.7\n73\t    - - Standard\n74\t      - 20%\n75\t      - 1.4\n76\t      - '[0.7, 2.4]'\n77\t      - 1.7\n78\t  figures_description:\n79\t  - 'Bar chart with 95% CIs showing the three conditions. Y-axis: GSM8K accuracy (%).\n80\t    Visual emphasis on the 27.5pp gap between RASP and Standard.'\n81\t  analysis: 'Full-set evaluation tightens CIs from ±9pp (N=100) to ±2.4pp, establishing\n82\t    statistical robustness. The 27.5pp gap is not marginal—it''s qualitative: RASP\n83\t    preserves partial reasoning capability (35% retention) while Standard is near-chance.\n84\t    This gap is the foundation for Claim 1: it determines whether recovery is possible\n85\t    at all. The baseline at 83.2% also establishes the ceiling for recovery experiments.\n86\t\n87\t    '\n88\t- name: SFT Warmup Recovery (RASP_20 + Standard_20)\n89\t  plan_id: plan_002\n90\t  description: 'Supervised fine-tuning on GSM8K train gold solutions (teacher-forcing)\n91\t    to restore basic generation ability in pruned models. Fixes the no-EOS degeneration\n92\t    problem observed in direct GRPO attempts. Both models trained in parallel with\n93\t    identical hyperparameters and data budget.\n94\t\n95\t    '\n96\t  expected_results:\n97\t    rasp_20_post_sft_gsm8k: 0.482\n98\t    rasp_20_post_sft_ci95:\n99\t    - 0.455\n100\t    - 0.509\n101\t    standard_20_post_sft_gsm8k: 0.147\n102\t    standard_20_post_sft_ci95:\n103\t    - 0.128\n104\t    - 0.168\n105\t    rasp_20_sft_gain_pp: 19.3\n106\t    standard_20_sft_gain_pp: 13.3\n107\t    rasp_20_eos_rate: 0.97\n108\t    standard_20_eos_rate: 0.93\n109\t    rasp_20_avg_response_length_tokens: 285\n110\t    standard_20_avg_response_length_tokens: 342\n111\t    sft_training_loss_rasp_final: 0.82\n112\t    sft_training_loss_standard_final: 1.41\n113\t    sft_epochs: 3\n114\t    sft_learning_rate: 2.0e-05\n115\t    sft_data_size: 7473\n116\t  result_rationale: 'SFT warmup serves two functions: (1) fix generation pathology\n117\t    (no-EOS infinite loops) by re-learning output format from gold solutions, and\n118\t    (2) provide a structured reasoning template for subsequent GRPO optimization.\n119\t    RASP_20 reaches ~48% because its preserved reasoning scaffolding (functionally\n120\t    unique heads) can absorb gold-solution supervision—SFT \"expands\" correct paths\n121\t    by imitation, and RASP''s intact reasoning structures provide the substrate for\n122\t    this expansion. Standard_20 reaches only ~15% because SFT can teach output format\n123\t    but cannot reconstruct the destroyed reasoning scaffold—it''s learning surface\n124\t    patterns without the underlying multi-step inference machinery. The 33pp gap after\n125\t    SFT (48% vs 15%) is wider than post-prune (29% vs 1%) in absolute terms because\n126\t    SFT amplifies the structural advantage: models with intact scaffolding benefit\n127\t    more from supervised reasoning demonstrations. Training loss confirms this—RASP\n128\t    converges to 0.82 (learning the reasoning patterns) while Standard plateaus at\n129\t    1.41 (unable to fit multi-step solutions without the structural capacity). Both\n130\t    models achieve >93% EOS rate, confirming SFT fixes generation pathology regardless\n131\t    of pruning method.\n132\t\n133\t    '\n134\t  tables:\n135\t  - name: 'Table 2: Post-SFT GSM8K Accuracy'\n136\t    columns:\n137\t    - Method\n138\t    - Post-Prune (%)\n139\t    - Post-SFT (%)\n140\t    - SFT Gain (pp)\n141\t    - EOS Rate (%)\n142\t    rows:\n143\t    - - RASP_20\n144\t      - 28.9\n145\t      - 48.2\n146\t      - 19.3\n147\t      - 97\n148\t    - - Standard_20\n149\t      - 1.4\n150\t      - 14.7\n151\t      - 13.3\n152\t      - 93\n153\t  figures_description:\n154\t  - Training loss curves (RASP vs Standard) over SFT steps, showing RASP's faster\n155\t    convergence and lower final loss. Include EOS rate subplot showing both models\n156\t    recovering proper termination.\n157\t  analysis: 'SFT-only results (48.2% vs 14.7%) already demonstrate RASP''s advantage,\n158\t    but the critical observation is that SFT alone cannot close the gap to baseline—RASP\n159\t    reaches 58% of baseline (48.2/83.2) while Standard reaches only 18%. This motivates\n160\t    GRPO: SFT provides the generation scaffold, GRPO optimizes reasoning paths. The\n161\t    \"SFT Expands\" mechanism is visible here—RASP''s preserved unique structures absorb\n162\t    supervision efficiently, while Standard''s destroyed scaffold limits SFT''s ceiling.\n163\t\n164\t    '\n165\t- name: GRPO Recovery on SFT-Warmed Models\n166\t  plan_id: plan_003\n167\t  description: 'Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) recovery on SFT-warmed RASP_20\n168\t    and Standard_20 models. Core MVP-completion experiment demonstrating that RASP\n169\t    enables RLVR recovery while Standard cannot meaningfully recover. Both models\n170\t    trained with identical GRPO hyperparameters (max_completion_length=4096 for R1-style\n171\t    long CoT).\n172\t\n173\t    '\n174\t  expected_results:\n175\t    rasp_20_sft_grpo_gsm8k: 0.721\n176\t    rasp_20_sft_grpo_ci95:\n177\t    - 0.697\n178\t    - 0.744\n179\t    standard_20_sft_grpo_gsm8k: 0.124\n180\t    standard_20_sft_grpo_ci95:\n181\t    - 0.107\n182\t    - 0.143\n183\t    rasp_recovery_rate: 0.867\n184\t    standard_recovery_rate: 0.149\n185\t    rasp_grpo_gain_over_sft_pp: 23.9\n186\t    standard_grpo_gain_over_sft_pp: -2.3\n187\t    rasp_grpo_reward_mean_final: 0.68\n188\t    standard_grpo_reward_mean_final: 0.11\n189\t    rasp_grpo_reward_std_final: 0.22\n190\t    standard_grpo_reward_std_final: 0.08\n191\t    rasp_grpo_kl_divergence_final: 3.2\n192\t    standard_grpo_kl_divergence_final: 0.9\n193\t    grpo_training_steps: 2000\n194\t    grpo_group_size: 8\n195\t    grpo_learning_rate: 5.0e-07\n196\t    grpo_max_completion_length: 4096\n197\t  result_rationale: 'GRPO recovery is where the Redundancy Paradox manifests most\n198\t    clearly. RASP_20 jumps from 48.2% (post-SFT) to 72.1%—a 23.9pp gain—because GRPO''s\n199\t    exploration-based optimization (\"RL Squeezes\") can discover and reinforce alternative\n200\t    reasoning paths through the retained structures. The key mechanism: RASP preserved\n201\t    functionally unique heads (low CKA overlap), which serve as irreplaceable anchors\n202\t    for reasoning chains. GRPO redistributes the function of pruned high-overlap heads\n203\t    across these anchors, effectively compressing the reasoning computation. The 72.1%\n204\t    represents 86.7% of baseline (72.1/83.2), consistent with the \"near-baseline recovery\"\n205\t    thesis. Standard_20 shows no GRPO benefit (14.7→12.4%, actually declining 2.3pp)\n206\t    because the destroyed reasoning scaffold provides no structural substrate for\n207\t    GRPO to optimize—there are no functionally unique reasoning anchors to redistribute\n208\t    through. GRPO''s exploration finds no rewarding paths because the model lacks\n209\t    the representational capacity for multi-step reasoning. The slight decline reflects\n210\t    GRPO''s KL penalty destabilizing the fragile SFT-learned patterns without discovering\n211\t    better alternatives. The reward signal confirms this: RASP achieves mean reward\n212\t    0.68 (finding correct solutions), while Standard plateaus at 0.11 (near-random).\n213\t    RASP''s higher KL (3.2 vs 0.9) indicates genuine policy movement—the model is\n214\t    exploring and finding better reasoning paths. Standard''s low KL means it barely\n215\t    moves from SFT initialization because exploration is unrewarding.\n216\t\n217\t    '\n218\t  tables:\n219\t  - name: 'Table 3: SFT→GRPO Recovery Pipeline Results'\n220\t    columns:\n221\t    - Method\n222\t    - Post-Prune (%)\n223\t    - Post-SFT (%)\n224\t    - Post-GRPO (%)\n225\t    - Recovery Rate (%)\n226\t    rows:\n227\t    - - RASP_20\n228\t      - 28.9\n229\t      - 48.2\n230\t      - 72.1\n231\t      - 86.7\n232\t    - - Standard_20\n233\t      - 1.4\n234\t      - 14.7\n235\t      - 12.4\n236\t      - 14.9\n237\t    - - Baseline\n238\t      - 83.2\n239\t      - —\n240\t      - —\n241\t      - —\n242\t  figures_description:\n243\t  - GRPO reward curves over training steps for RASP_20 vs Standard_20. RASP shows\n244\t    steady climb to 0.68; Standard flatlines near 0.11. Include KL divergence subplot.\n245\t  - Waterfall chart showing accuracy at each pipeline stage (post-prune → post-SFT\n246\t    → post-GRPO) for both methods. Visual narrative of RASP's progressive recovery\n247\t    vs Standard's ceiling.\n248\t  analysis: 'This is the headline result. RASP enables 86.7% recovery while Standard\n249\t    achieves only 14.9%—a qualitative difference between \"recoverable\" and \"unrecoverable.\"\n250\t    The GRPO stage contributes +23.9pp for RASP but -2.3pp for Standard, demonstrating\n251\t    that RLVR recovery requires structural prerequisites that only CKA-aware pruning\n252\t    preserves. The \"RL Squeezes\" mechanism is confirmed: GRPO compresses reasoning\n253\t    through retained unique structures, which requires those structures to exist.\n254\t    This supports Claim 1 definitively and motivates Claims 2-3.\n255\t\n256\t    '\n257\t- name: Full GSM8K Eval on All Recovered Models\n258\t  plan_id: plan_004\n259\t  description: 'Publication-quality 1319-sample GSM8K evaluation on all recovered\n260\t    models from plan_003. Final numbers for paper Table 1 with bootstrapped CIs.\n261\t\n262\t    '\n263\t  expected_results:\n264\t    rasp_20_recovered_gsm8k: 0.721\n265\t    rasp_20_recovered_ci95_bootstrap:\n266\t    - 0.694\n267\t    - 0.746\n268\t    standard_20_recovered_gsm8k: 0.124\n269\t    standard_20_recovered_ci95_bootstrap:\n270\t    - 0.104\n271\t    - 0.146\n272\t    rasp_vs_standard_recovered_gap_pp: 59.7\n273\t    effect_size_cohens_h: 1.28\n274\t    rasp_recovery_fraction_of_baseline: 0.867\n275\t    standard_recovery_fraction_of_baseline: 0.149\n276\t    n_samples: 1319\n277\t    bootstrap_iterations: 10000\n278\t  result_rationale: 'Full-set evaluation with bootstrap CIs confirms the GRPO results\n279\t    are not artifacts of evaluation noise. The 59.7pp gap between recovered models\n280\t    (72.1% vs 12.4%) is even larger than the post-prune gap (27.5pp), demonstrating\n281\t    that the recovery pipeline amplifies the structural advantage. Cohen''s h of 1.28\n282\t    is a \"very large\" effect size, ruling out any marginal-improvement narrative.\n283\t    Bootstrap CIs (10K iterations) provide non-parametric confidence bounds suitable\n284\t    for publication. These numbers populate the paper''s main results table and abstract.\n285\t\n286\t    '\n287\t  tables:\n288\t  - name: 'Table 1 (Paper): Main Results at 20% Structured Sparsity'\n289\t    columns:\n290\t    - Method\n291\t    - Post-Prune (%)\n292\t    - Post-SFT (%)\n293\t    - Post-SFT+GRPO (%)\n294\t    - Recovery (%)\n295\t    - 95% CI\n296\t    rows:\n297\t    - - Baseline\n298\t      - '83.2'\n299\t      - —\n300\t      - —\n301\t      - —\n302\t      - '[81.1, 85.2]'\n303\t    - - RASP\n304\t      - '28.9'\n305\t      - '48.2'\n306\t      - '72.1'\n307\t      - '86.7'\n308\t      - '[69.4, 74.6]'\n309\t    - - Standard\n310\t      - '1.4'\n311\t      - '14.7'\n312\t      - '12.4'\n313\t      - '14.9'\n314\t      - '[10.4, 14.6]'\n315\t    - - GRASPrune\n316\t      - 17.8†\n317\t      - 32.6†\n318\t      - 41.3†\n319\t      - 49.6†\n320\t      - —\n321\t  figures_description:\n322\t  - 'Grouped bar chart: post-prune vs post-recovery accuracy for all methods. Arrows\n323\t    showing recovery magnitude. This is Figure 1 of the paper.'\n324\t  analysis: 'Table 1 is the paper''s anchor result. The narrative is clear: same model,\n325\t    same sparsity, same recovery budget—only the pruning criterion differs, and it\n326\t    determines whether recovery succeeds (87%) or fails (15%). The GRASPrune row (plan_005,\n327\t    marked with †) will be added once that experiment completes.\n328\t\n329\t    '\n330\t- name: GRASPrune Baseline at 20% Sparsity\n331\t  plan_id: plan_005\n332\t  description: 'Full pipeline for GRASPrune (ACL 2026 SOTA budgeted pruning) at 20%\n333\t    sparsity: gate training → pruning → eval → SFT warmup → GRPO recovery. Primary\n334\t    external baseline to demonstrate that importance-aware pruning without CKA is\n335\t    insufficient.\n336\t\n337\t    '\n338\t  expected_results:\n339\t    graspune_post_prune_gsm8k: 0.178\n340\t    graspune_post_prune_ci95:\n341\t    - 0.157\n342\t    - 0.2\n343\t    graspune_post_sft_gsm8k: 0.326\n344\t    graspune_post_sft_grpo_gsm8k: 0.413\n345\t    graspune_post_sft_grpo_ci95:\n346\t    - 0.386\n347\t    - 0.441\n348\t    graspune_recovery_rate: 0.496\n349\t    graspune_gate_training_epochs: 5\n350\t    graspune_gate_loss_final: 0.34\n351\t    graspune_vs_rasp_post_prune_gap_pp: -11.1\n352\t    graspune_vs_rasp_recovered_gap_pp: -30.8\n353\t  result_rationale: 'GRASPrune at 17.8% post-prune sits between RASP (28.9%) and Standard\n354\t    (1.4%), confirming it as the right intermediate baseline. GRASPrune''s learned\n355\t    gates provide better importance estimation than static metrics (explaining its\n356\t    advantage over Standard), but without CKA-awareness it still removes some functionally\n357\t    unique reasoning heads. Post-prune gap to RASP is 11.1pp— substantial but not\n358\t    as dramatic as the 27.5pp RASP-Standard gap. The critical observation emerges\n359\t    in recovery: GRASPrune recovers to only 41.3% (49.6% of baseline) vs RASP''s 72.1%\n360\t    (86.7%). The recovery gap (30.8pp) is nearly 3× the post-prune gap (11.1pp), demonstrating\n361\t    that recovery amplifies structural choices—CKA-unaware pruning destroys some irreplaceable\n362\t    scaffolding that GRPO cannot recover. GRASPrune''s gate training does capture\n363\t    some functional redundancy implicitly (through gradient flow), explaining its\n364\t    partial recoverability, but the explicit CKA signal in RASP provides superior\n365\t    structure selection. This confirms the paper''s core argument: CKA overlap is\n366\t    the \"missing ingredient\" that separates good importance estimation from recovery-optimal\n367\t    pruning.\n368\t\n369\t    '\n370\t  tables:\n371\t  - name: 'Table: GRASPrune Pipeline Results'\n372\t    columns:\n373\t    - Method\n374\t    - Post-Prune (%)\n375\t    - Post-SFT (%)\n376\t    - Post-GRPO (%)\n377\t    - Recovery (%)\n378\t    rows:\n379\t    - - RASP\n380\t      - 28.9\n381\t      - 48.2\n382\t      - 72.1\n383\t      - 86.7\n384\t    - - GRASPrune\n385\t      - 17.8\n386\t      - 32.6\n387\t      - 41.3\n388\t      - 49.6\n389\t    - - Standard\n390\t      - 1.4\n391\t      - 14.7\n392\t      - 12.4\n393\t      - 14.9\n394\t  figures_description:\n395\t  - Three-method comparison bar chart at each pipeline stage. GRASPrune clearly intermediate,\n396\t    confirming the ablation narrative.\n397\t  - 'Scatter plot: per-head CKA overlap (x) vs heads retained by each method (color).\n398\t    Shows RASP retains low-F(h) heads that GRASPrune partially removes.'\n399\t  analysis: 'GRASPrune as intermediate baseline is essential for the paper''s argument.\n400\t    It breaks the potential confound that RASP''s advantage is simply better importance\n401\t    estimation—GRASPrune has arguably better importance estimation (learned gates\n402\t    vs static Taylor) but worse recovery. The 30.8pp recovery gap vs only 11.1pp post-prune\n403\t    gap quantifies the \"CKA premium\" in recovery-aware pruning. Reviewers asking \"is\n404\t    this just a better pruning metric?\" are answered directly: no, CKA captures something\n405\t    orthogonal to importance that specifically enables RLVR recovery.\n406\t\n407\t    '\n408\t- name: Multi-Seed RASP_20 Post-Prune Evaluation\n409\t  plan_id: plan_006\n410\t  description: 'RASP pruning and evaluation at 20% sparsity with seeds 123 and 7 (original\n411\t    was seed 42). Tests robustness of the 29% post-prune result across different CKA\n412\t    calibration samples and pruning tie-breaking.\n413\t\n414\t    '\n415\t  expected_results:\n416\t    seed_42_gsm8k: 0.289\n417\t    seed_123_gsm8k: 0.281\n418\t    seed_7_gsm8k: 0.302\n419\t    mean_across_seeds: 0.291\n420\t    std_across_seeds: 0.011\n421\t    cv_across_seeds: 0.037\n422\t    max_min_range_pp: 2.1\n423\t    seed_123_overlap_with_42_pruned_set: 0.91\n424\t    seed_7_overlap_with_42_pruned_set: 0.89\n425\t  result_rationale: 'RASP''s scoring is deterministic given fixed calibration data—seed\n426\t    variation affects only the GSM8K calibration sample selection (512 of 7473 train\n427\t    samples). Since CKA overlap is computed over 512 mean-pooled activations and averaged\n428\t    across all intra-layer pairs, the statistic is stable: different calibration subsets\n429\t    sample from the same activation distribution. Seed 123 yields 28.1% (slightly\n430\t    lower due to a calibration set with fewer multi-step problems, marginally shifting\n431\t    one borderline head''s F(h) across the pruning threshold in layer 14). Seed 7\n432\t    yields 30.2% (calibration set slightly enriched in diverse problem types, producing\n433\t    sharper CKA discrimination). Standard deviation of 1.1pp across 3 seeds is well\n434\t    within the [0, 3]pp best-tier bound, confirming that the 29% result is not seed-dependent.\n435\t    The 89-91% overlap in pruned head sets across seeds shows that RASP''s structure\n436\t    selection is highly stable—the same ~179 heads are pruned regardless of calibration\n437\t    seed, with only 16-20 heads varying at the margin. This rules out the \"lucky seed\"\n438\t    concern.\n439\t\n440\t    '\n441\t  tables:\n442\t  - name: 'Table: Multi-Seed Robustness'\n443\t    columns:\n444\t    - Seed\n445\t    - GSM8K (%)\n446\t    - Pruned Set Overlap w/ Seed 42 (%)\n447\t    rows:\n448\t    - - 42\n449\t      - 28.9\n450\t      - —\n451\t    - - 123\n452\t      - 28.1\n453\t      - 91\n454\t    - - 7\n455\t      - 30.2\n456\t      - 89\n457\t    - - Mean ± Std\n458\t      - 29.1 ± 1.1\n459\t      - —\n460\t  figures_description:\n461\t  - Strip plot with three seed points and mean±std band. Demonstrates low variance\n462\t    and confirms robustness.\n463\t  analysis: 'σ=1.1pp across seeds establishes that RASP''s structural advantage is\n464\t    robust, not a calibration artifact. The high overlap in pruned sets (89-91%) shows\n465\t    the ranking is stable. This is a necessary control for Claim 1—without it, reviewers\n466\t    could dismiss the 29% result as seed-specific. Combined with full-set evaluation\n467\t    (plan_001), this provides both precision and robustness.\n468\t\n469\t    '\n470\t- name: Scoring Ablation at 20% Sparsity\n471\t  plan_id: plan_007\n472\t  description: 'Controlled ablation of the RASP scoring function. Four conditions\n473\t    at 20% sparsity: magnitude-only (L2 norm), gradient-only (Taylor expansion = I(h)),\n474\t    random pruning, and CKA-only (F(h) without importance weighting). Isolates the\n475\t    contribution of each scoring component.\n476\t\n477\t    '\n478\t  expected_results:\n479\t    magnitude_only_gsm8k: 0.038\n480\t    gradient_only_gsm8k: 0.014\n481\t    random_pruning_gsm8k: 0.067\n482\t    cka_only_gsm8k: 0.213\n483\t    rasp_full_gsm8k: 0.289\n484\t    standard_importance_gsm8k: 0.014\n485\t    magnitude_only_ci95:\n486\t    - 0.023\n487\t    - 0.058\n488\t    gradient_only_ci95:\n489\t    - 0.007\n490\t    - 0.024\n491\t    random_pruning_ci95:\n492\t    - 0.049\n493\t    - 0.089\n494\t    cka_only_ci95:\n495\t    - 0.191\n496\t    - 0.237\n497\t  result_rationale: 'This ablation reveals a surprising finding: CKA-only scoring\n498\t    (21.3%) vastly outperforms all importance-only variants (1.4-3.8%), and even random\n499\t    pruning (6.7%) beats importance-based methods. The explanation crystallizes the\n500\t    Redundancy Paradox. Importance-only methods (magnitude=3.8%, gradient=1.4%) remove\n501\t    \"unimportant\" structures that happen to be functionally unique— these structures\n502\t    carry specialized reasoning functions not reflected in simple importance metrics.\n503\t    By contrast, CKA-only scoring protects unique structures regardless of measured\n504\t    importance, preserving the reasoning scaffold. Random pruning at 6.7% outperforms\n505\t    importance-based methods because random selection has no bias toward removing\n506\t    unique structures— it removes high-overlap and low-overlap structures equiprobably.\n507\t    Gradient-only (1.4%) matches Standard because Standard IS gradient-based Taylor\n508\t    importance (I(h)). Magnitude-only (3.8%) does slightly better because L2 norm\n509\t    captures structural capacity more broadly. The full RASP score (28.9%) outperforms\n510\t    CKA-only (21.3%) by 7.6pp because importance weighting correctly identifies which\n511\t    unique structures are also functionally important—pure CKA protection without\n512\t    importance wastes budget retaining unique-but-useless structures. This confirms\n513\t    both components contribute to the RASP score, but CKA is the dominant factor (21.3%\n514\t    vs 1.4-3.8% for importance variants), supporting Claim 2.\n515\t\n516\t    '\n517\t  tables:\n518\t  - name: 'Table: Scoring Ablation at 20% Sparsity'\n519\t    columns:\n520\t    - Scoring Method\n521\t    - Formula\n522\t    - GSM8K (%)\n523\t    - 95% CI\n524\t    rows:\n525\t    - - RASP (full)\n526\t      - I(h) × (1-F(h))^α\n527\t      - 28.9\n528\t      - '[26.5, 31.4]'\n529\t    - - CKA-only\n530\t      - (1-F(h))^α\n531\t      - 21.3\n532\t      - '[19.1, 23.7]'\n533\t    - - Random\n534\t      - Uniform random\n535\t      - 6.7\n536\t      - '[4.9, 8.9]'\n537\t    - - Magnitude-only\n538\t      - '||w_h||₂'\n539\t      - 3.8\n540\t      - '[2.3, 5.8]'\n541\t    - - Gradient-only\n542\t      - '||w_h ⊙ ∇w_h||₂'\n543\t      - 1.4\n544\t      - '[0.7, 2.4]'\n545\t    - - Standard (= Gradient)\n546\t      - I(h) ascending\n547\t      - 1.4\n548\t      - '[0.7, 2.4]'\n549\t  figures_description:\n550\t  - 'Horizontal bar chart ranked by accuracy. Clear visual hierarchy: RASP > CKA-only\n551\t    >> Random >> Magnitude > Gradient = Standard. Annotate the ''Redundancy Paradox''\n552\t    region where importance methods underperform random.'\n553\t  analysis: 'The ablation provides direct evidence for the Redundancy Paradox: importance-\n554\t    based pruning is worse than random for reasoning model compression. This is because\n555\t    importance metrics are anti-correlated with functional uniqueness in reasoning\n556\t    LLMs—\"important\" heads tend to be high-overlap (redundant) heads that contribute\n557\t    to many reasoning paths, while \"unimportant\" heads may be specialized for specific\n558\t    reasoning operations. CKA''s dominance (21.3% vs 3.8%) confirms that functional\n559\t    overlap is the primary signal for reasoning preservation. The RASP premium over\n560\t    CKA-only (+7.6pp) shows importance still contributes when combined with CKA.\n561\t\n562\t    '\n563\t- name: Per-Structure CKA-Recoverability Correlation\n564\t  plan_id: plan_008\n565\t  description: 'Mixed-effects regression analysis at the per-head level. For each\n566\t    of 896 attention heads, extract: CKA overlap F(h), importance I(h), weight norm,\n567\t    gradient magnitude, activation magnitude, and post-recovery activation change\n568\t    (recoverability proxy). Layer as random effect.\n569\t\n570\t    '\n571\t  expected_results:\n572\t    n_heads_total: 896\n573\t    n_layers: 32\n574\t    mixed_effects_r2_marginal: 0.37\n575\t    mixed_effects_r2_conditional: 0.58\n576\t    cka_overlap_beta: 0.42\n577\t    cka_overlap_se: 0.06\n578\t    cka_overlap_p: <0.0001\n579\t    weight_norm_beta: 0.08\n580\t    weight_norm_p: 0.12\n581\t    gradient_importance_beta: 0.11\n582\t    gradient_importance_p: 0.034\n583\t    activation_magnitude_beta: 0.05\n584\t    activation_magnitude_p: 0.31\n585\t    cka_partial_r2: 0.22\n586\t    importance_partial_r2: 0.04\n587\t    layer_random_effect_variance: 0.15\n588\t    icc: 0.21\n589\t    vif_max: 2.8\n590\t    durbin_watson: 1.89\n591\t    breusch_pagan_p: 0.18\n592\t  result_rationale: 'Mixed-effects regression with marginal R²=0.37 validates CKA\n593\t    overlap as a meaningful predictor of recoverability, exceeding the [0.25, 0.45]\n594\t    best-tier bound comfortably. The key finding: CKA overlap''s standardized β=0.42\n595\t    (p<0.0001) dominates all other predictors—weight norm (β=0.08, n.s.), gradient\n596\t    importance (β=0.11, marginal), and activation magnitude (β=0.05, n.s.) are comparatively\n597\t    weak. Partial R² for CKA alone is 0.22, meaning CKA uniquely explains 22% of recoverability\n598\t    variance beyond what other metrics capture. This is because CKA overlap measures\n599\t    functional redundancy— the degree to which a head''s computation is replicated\n600\t    elsewhere in the layer—which directly predicts whether GRPO can redistribute that\n601\t    head''s function. Importance metrics measure current contribution but not redistributability.\n602\t    The conditional R²=0.58 (vs marginal 0.37) shows layer identity explains additional\n603\t    variance—early layers (0-5) have lower recoverability per unit of CKA overlap\n604\t    (their functions are more position- specific), while middle layers (10-20) show\n605\t    strongest CKA-recoverability coupling (most reasoning redistribution occurs here).\n606\t    ICC=0.21 confirms non-trivial layer clustering, justifying the mixed-effects specification.\n607\t    Diagnostics are clean: VIF<3 (no multicollinearity), Durbin-Watson≈2 (no autocorrelation),\n608\t    Breusch-Pagan n.s. (homoscedastic residuals).\n609\t\n610\t    '\n611\t  tables:\n612\t  - name: 'Table: Mixed-Effects Regression on Per-Head Recoverability'\n613\t    columns:\n614\t    - Predictor\n615\t    - β (std)\n616\t    - SE\n617\t    - t\n618\t    - p\n619\t    - Partial R²\n620\t    rows:\n621\t    - - F(h) (CKA overlap)\n622\t      - 0.42\n623\t      - 0.06\n624\t      - 7.0\n625\t      - <0.0001\n626\t      - 0.22\n627\t    - - I(h) (gradient importance)\n628\t      - 0.11\n629\t      - 0.05\n630\t      - 2.13\n631\t      - 0.034\n632\t      - 0.04\n633\t    - - '||w_h||₂ (weight norm)'\n634\t      - 0.08\n635\t      - 0.05\n636\t      - 1.55\n637\t      - 0.12\n638\t      - 0.01\n639\t    - - Act. magnitude\n640\t      - 0.05\n641\t      - 0.05\n642\t      - 1.02\n643\t      - 0.31\n644\t      - <0.01\n645\t    - - —\n646\t      - —\n647\t      - —\n648\t      - —\n649\t      - —\n650\t      - —\n651\t    - - Marginal R²\n652\t      - 0.37\n653\t      - —\n654\t      - —\n655\t      - —\n656\t      - —\n657\t    - - Conditional R²\n658\t      - 0.58\n659\t      - —\n660\t      - —\n661\t      - —\n662\t      - —\n663\t    - - ICC (layer)\n664\t      - 0.21\n665\t      - —\n666\t      - —\n667\t      - —\n668\t      - —\n669\t  figures_description:\n670\t  - 'Scatter plot: per-head F(h) (x-axis) vs recoverability (y-axis), colored by layer\n671\t    depth. Marginal regression line with 95% prediction band. Shows positive relationship\n672\t    with layer-dependent intercept variation.'\n673\t  - Coefficient plot with 95% CIs for all predictors. CKA overlap clearly separated\n674\t    from zero; other predictors near zero or marginal.\n675\t  - Layer-specific random effect estimates (caterpillar plot). Shows middle layers\n676\t    have highest intercepts, early layers lowest.\n677\t  analysis: 'This is the core evidence for Claim 2. CKA overlap (β=0.42) is 4× stronger\n678\t    than the next-best predictor (gradient importance, β=0.11) and uniquely explains\n679\t    22% of recoverability variance. The marginal R²=0.37 means CKA + standard metrics\n680\t    explain 37% of recovery outcomes—substantial for a single- model per-head analysis.\n681\t    The layer random effects (ICC=0.21) reveal mechanistic structure: reasoning redistribution\n682\t    is concentrated in middle layers (10-20), consistent with the \"RL Squeezes\" mechanism\n683\t    operating on the reasoning computation core. This validates RASP''s design: using\n684\t    F(h) to guide pruning decisions captures a signal about post-recovery behavior\n685\t    that standard importance metrics miss.\n686\t\n687\t    '\n688\t- name: SFT-Only Recovery Comparison\n689\t  plan_id: plan_009\n690\t  description: 'Same pruned models (RASP_20, Standard_20), same data budget, but SFT-only\n691\t    recovery (no GRPO). Tests Claim 3: whether recovery method changes optimal pruning\n692\t    order. Combined with plan_003 results, enables 2×2 interaction analysis.\n693\t\n694\t    '\n695\t  expected_results:\n696\t    sft_only_rasp_gsm8k: 0.482\n697\t    sft_only_standard_gsm8k: 0.147\n698\t    sft_only_rasp_ci95:\n699\t    - 0.455\n700\t    - 0.509\n701\t    sft_only_standard_ci95:\n702\t    - 0.128\n703\t    - 0.168\n704\t    sft_grpo_rasp_gsm8k: 0.721\n705\t    sft_grpo_standard_gsm8k: 0.124\n706\t    grpo_marginal_gain_rasp_pp: 23.9\n707\t    grpo_marginal_gain_standard_pp: -2.3\n708\t    interaction_term_p: 0.0023\n709\t    interaction_effect_size_pp: 26.2\n710\t    rasp_advantage_under_sft_pp: 33.5\n711\t    rasp_advantage_under_grpo_pp: 59.7\n712\t    rasp_rank_sft: 1\n713\t    rasp_rank_grpo: 1\n714\t    standard_sft_recovery_rate: 0.177\n715\t    standard_grpo_recovery_rate: 0.149\n716\t  result_rationale: 'SFT-only recovery reveals the pruning×method interaction central\n717\t    to Claim 3. Under SFT-only recovery, RASP''s advantage is 33.5pp (48.2% vs 14.7%)—\n718\t    significant but not catastrophic for Standard. Under SFT+GRPO, RASP''s advantage\n719\t    explodes to 59.7pp (72.1% vs 12.4%). The interaction is driven by GRPO''s asymmetric\n720\t    effect: +23.9pp for RASP but -2.3pp for Standard. The mechanism is \"RL Squeezes,\n721\t    SFT Expands\": SFT recovery works by expanding correct solution paths through imitation—it\n722\t    benefits from any preserved structure and doesn''t strongly depend on functional\n723\t    redundancy patterns. Both RASP and Standard benefit from SFT proportionally to\n724\t    their preserved capacity. GRPO recovery works by compressing incorrect paths and\n725\t    reinforcing correct ones through exploration—it requires functional redundancy\n726\t    (overlapping structures whose function can be redistributed) to find alternative\n727\t    reasoning routes. RASP preserves this redundancy by design; Standard destroys\n728\t    it. The interaction p=0.0023 is highly significant, and the 26.2pp interaction\n729\t    effect size confirms that optimal pruning strategy genuinely depends on the intended\n730\t    recovery method. Importantly, Standard under SFT-only (14.7%) slightly outperforms\n731\t    Standard under GRPO (12.4%), suggesting that GRPO can actively harm models without\n732\t    the structural prerequisites for RL-based recovery—the KL penalty destabilizes\n733\t    learned patterns without discovering better alternatives.\n734\t\n735\t    '\n736\t  tables:\n737\t  - name: 'Table: Pruning × Recovery Method Interaction (2×2)'\n738\t    columns:\n739\t    - Pruning\n740\t    - SFT-Only (%)\n741\t    - SFT+GRPO (%)\n742\t    - GRPO Marginal (pp)\n743\t    rows:\n744\t    - - RASP\n745\t      - 48.2\n746\t      - 72.1\n747\t      - 23.9\n748\t    - - Standard\n749\t      - 14.7\n750\t      - 12.4\n751\t      - -2.3\n752\t    - - RASP Advantage (pp)\n753\t      - 33.5\n754\t      - 59.7\n755\t      - —\n756\t  - name: 'Table: Interaction Test'\n757\t    columns:\n758\t    - Effect\n759\t    - Estimate (pp)\n760\t    - SE\n761\t    - p\n762\t    rows:\n763\t    - - Pruning (RASP vs Std)\n764\t      - 46.6\n765\t      - 3.2\n766\t      - <0.0001\n767\t    - - Method (GRPO vs SFT)\n768\t      - 10.8\n769\t      - 3.1\n770\t      - '0.0006'\n771\t    - - Pruning × Method\n772\t      - 26.2\n773\t      - 4.5\n774\t      - '0.0023'\n775\t  figures_description:\n776\t  - '2×2 interaction plot: x-axis = recovery method (SFT, SFT+GRPO), y-axis = GSM8K\n777\t    accuracy, separate lines for RASP and Standard. Non-parallel lines demonstrate\n778\t    the interaction. This is Figure 3 of the paper.'\n779\t  - 'Marginal GRPO gain comparison: paired bar chart showing +23.9pp (RASP) vs -2.3pp\n780\t    (Standard). Visual emphasis on the divergent GRPO effect.'\n781\t  analysis: 'The interaction effect (p=0.0023, 26.2pp) directly supports Claim 3.\n782\t    The story is compelling: SFT recovery is method-agnostic (benefits from any preserved\n783\t    structure), while GRPO recovery is structure-dependent (requires the specific\n784\t    redundancy pattern that RASP preserves). This means the optimal pruning strategy\n785\t    depends on the intended recovery method—a novel finding not predicted by existing\n786\t    pruning or recovery literature. The negative GRPO marginal for Standard (-2.3pp)\n787\t    strengthens the narrative: GRPO doesn''t just fail to help Standard-pruned models,\n788\t    it actively hurts them.\n789\t\n790\t    '\n791\t- name: 25% Sparsity RASP + Standard\n792\t  plan_id: plan_010\n793\t  description: 'Full pipeline (prune → eval → SFT → GRPO → eval) at 25% sparsity for\n794\t    both RASP and Standard. Tests robustness across sparsity levels and whether RASP''s\n795\t    advantage narrows or widens at higher compression.\n796\t\n797\t    '\n798\t  expected_results:\n799\t    rasp_25_post_prune_gsm8k: 0.184\n800\t    rasp_25_post_prune_ci95:\n801\t    - 0.164\n802\t    - 0.206\n803\t    standard_25_post_prune_gsm8k: 0.003\n804\t    standard_25_post_prune_ci95:\n805\t    - 0.001\n806\t    - 0.01\n807\t    rasp_25_post_sft_gsm8k: 0.356\n808\t    standard_25_post_sft_gsm8k: 0.068\n809\t    rasp_25_sft_grpo_gsm8k: 0.581\n810\t    rasp_25_sft_grpo_ci95:\n811\t    - 0.554\n812\t    - 0.607\n813\t    standard_25_sft_grpo_gsm8k: 0.054\n814\t    standard_25_sft_grpo_ci95:\n815\t    - 0.04\n816\t    - 0.07\n817\t    rasp_25_recovery_rate: 0.698\n818\t    standard_25_recovery_rate: 0.065\n819\t    rasp_25_vs_20_post_prune_delta_pp: -10.5\n820\t    rasp_25_vs_20_recovered_delta_pp: -14.0\n821\t  result_rationale: 'At 25% sparsity, RASP''s post-prune accuracy drops to 18.4% (from\n822\t    28.9% at 20%), losing ~10.5pp from the additional 5% structural removal. Standard\n823\t    drops to 0.3% (from 1.4%), essentially zero. The gap widens slightly in absolute\n824\t    terms (18.1pp vs 27.5pp at 20%) but the critical question is recoverability. RASP_25\n825\t    recovers to 58.1% (69.8% of baseline)—lower than RASP_20''s 72.1% but still demonstrating\n826\t    substantial recovery. The ~14pp drop from 20% to 25% reflects the removal of additional\n827\t    functionally unique structures that, while RASP tried to protect, had to be pruned\n828\t    to meet the sparsity target. Each additional unique head removed reduces the recovery\n829\t    ceiling because fewer anchors exist for GRPO''s redistribution mechanism. Standard_25\n830\t    at 5.4% post- recovery shows that additional pruning further destroys recovery\n831\t    potential. The 52.7pp gap at 25% (58.1% vs 5.4%) is comparable to the 59.7pp gap\n832\t    at 20% (72.1% vs 12.4%), confirming RASP''s advantage is robust across sparsity\n833\t    levels. The diminishing returns from 20→25% suggest a natural operating point:\n834\t    20% maximizes recovery quality for this model.\n835\t\n836\t    '\n837\t  tables:\n838\t  - name: 'Table: Sparsity Scaling (RASP vs Standard)'\n839\t    columns:\n840\t    - Sparsity\n841\t    - Method\n842\t    - Post-Prune (%)\n843\t    - Recovered (%)\n844\t    - Recovery Rate (%)\n845\t    rows:\n846\t    - - 20%\n847\t      - RASP\n848\t      - 28.9\n849\t      - 72.1\n850\t      - 86.7\n851\t    - - 20%\n852\t      - Standard\n853\t      - 1.4\n854\t      - 12.4\n855\t      - 14.9\n856\t    - - 25%\n857\t      - RASP\n858\t      - 18.4\n859\t      - 58.1\n860\t      - 69.8\n861\t    - - 25%\n862\t      - Standard\n863\t      - 0.3\n864\t      - 5.4\n865\t      - 6.5\n866\t  figures_description:\n867\t  - 'Line plot: sparsity (x-axis, 20% and 25%) vs recovered accuracy (y-axis), separate\n868\t    lines for RASP and Standard. Shows RASP''s graceful degradation vs Standard''s\n869\t    collapse. Extrapolation dashes toward 30%.'\n870\t  analysis: 'The 25% sparsity results confirm that RASP''s advantage is not an artifact\n871\t    of the specific 20% operating point. RASP maintains meaningful recoverability\n872\t    (69.8%) even at higher compression, while Standard''s already-minimal recovery\n873\t    degrades further. This multi-sparsity evidence strengthens the generalizability\n874\t    claim and provides practical guidance: RASP enables aggressive compression with\n875\t    recovery, while Standard collapses even at moderate sparsity.\n876\t\n877\t    '\n878\t- name: 30% Sparsity Probe\n879\t  plan_id: plan_011\n880\t  description: 'Prune + eval only (no recovery) for RASP and Standard at 30% sparsity.\n881\t    Maps the threshold where both methods collapse, contextualizing the operating\n882\t    range of the Redundancy Paradox.\n883\t\n884\t    '\n885\t  expected_results:\n886\t    rasp_30_post_prune_gsm8k: 0.062\n887\t    rasp_30_post_prune_ci95:\n888\t    - 0.045\n889\t    - 0.083\n890\t    standard_30_post_prune_gsm8k: 0.001\n891\t    standard_30_post_prune_ci95:\n892\t    - 0.0\n893\t    - 0.006\n894\t    rasp_30_n_unique_heads_remaining: 14\n895\t    standard_30_n_unique_heads_remaining: 3\n896\t  result_rationale: 'At 30% sparsity, RASP post-prune drops to 6.2%—still above zero\n897\t    but approaching the collapse zone observed at 35% (0% for both methods). This\n898\t    is consistent with the D005 finding: the DeepSeek-R1-Distill model, having been\n899\t    distilled from a larger model, has less structural redundancy than a natively-trained\n900\t    model of equivalent size. At 30%, even RASP must remove some functionally unique\n901\t    heads, leaving too few reasoning anchors for meaningful computation. Standard\n902\t    at 0.1% is effectively zero. The 30% probe establishes the upper bound of RASP''s\n903\t    operating range for this model and provides context for the 20-25% results: RASP''s\n904\t    advantage is most pronounced in the 20-25% range where it can still protect critical\n905\t    scaffolding. Beyond 30%, both methods fail because the model simply doesn''t have\n906\t    enough structural redundancy to support reasoning at that compression. We do not\n907\t    attempt recovery at 30% because the post-prune accuracy is too low (6.2%) to provide\n908\t    meaningful signal—plan_003 showed that GRPO requires a minimum functional baseline\n909\t    to discover rewarding paths.\n910\t\n911\t    '\n912\t  tables:\n913\t  - name: 'Table: Sparsity Scaling (Probe)'\n914\t    columns:\n915\t    - Sparsity\n916\t    - Method\n917\t    - Post-Prune (%)\n918\t    - Status\n919\t    rows:\n920\t    - - 20%\n921\t      - RASP\n922\t      - 28.9\n923\t      - Recoverable\n924\t    - - 25%\n925\t      - RASP\n926\t      - 18.4\n927\t      - Recoverable\n928\t    - - 30%\n929\t      - RASP\n930\t      - 6.2\n931\t      - Near-collapse\n932\t    - - 35%\n933\t      - RASP\n934\t      - 0.0\n935\t      - Collapsed (D005)\n936\t    - - 20%\n937\t      - Standard\n938\t      - 1.4\n939\t      - Collapsed\n940\t    - - 25%\n941\t      - Standard\n942\t      - 0.3\n943\t      - Collapsed\n944\t    - - 30%\n945\t      - Standard\n946\t      - 0.1\n947\t      - Collapsed\n948\t    - - 35%\n949\t      - Standard\n950\t      - 0.0\n951\t      - Collapsed (D005)\n952\t  figures_description:\n953\t  - 'Sparsity curve: x-axis sparsity (20-35%), y-axis post-prune GSM8K, lines for\n954\t    RASP and Standard. RASP shows graceful degradation with knee at ~28%; Standard\n955\t    is flat near zero. Shaded ''recoverable zone'' for RASP between 20-25%.'\n956\t  analysis: 'The sparsity curve reveals RASP''s operating envelope: meaningful reasoning\n957\t    preservation up to ~25%, rapid degradation at 30%, collapse at 35%. Standard collapses\n958\t    immediately at 20%. This positions RASP as enabling a ~25% \"recoverable sparsity\"\n959\t    window that doesn''t exist for importance-only pruning. The 35% collapse for both\n960\t    methods contextualizes the model''s inherent redundancy budget—distilled models\n961\t    have less slack than natively trained ones.\n962\t\n963\t    '\n964\t- name: Extended Evaluation on Best Recovered Config\n965\t  plan_id: plan_012\n966\t  description: 'Evaluate the best recovered model (RASP_20 SFT+GRPO) on MMLU (5-shot),\n967\t    HumanEval (pass@1), and MATH-500 to test generalization beyond GSM8K.\n968\t\n969\t    '\n970\t  expected_results:\n971\t    baseline_mmlu: 0.631\n972\t    rasp_recovered_mmlu: 0.584\n973\t    rasp_mmlu_retention: 0.926\n974\t    baseline_humaneval: 0.524\n975\t    rasp_recovered_humaneval: 0.451\n976\t    rasp_humaneval_retention: 0.861\n977\t    baseline_math500: 0.682\n978\t    rasp_recovered_math500: 0.571\n979\t    rasp_math500_retention: 0.837\n980\t    mean_retention_across_benchmarks: 0.875\n981\t    gsm8k_retention_for_reference: 0.867\n982\t  result_rationale: 'RASP recovery generalizes beyond GSM8K. MMLU retention (92.6%)\n983\t    is highest because MMLU primarily tests knowledge retrieval and pattern matching,\n984\t    which are less affected by structural pruning than multi-step reasoning—the retained\n985\t    structures preserve factual knowledge effectively. HumanEval retention (86.1%)\n986\t    is comparable to GSM8K (86.7%) because code generation requires similar multi-step\n987\t    reasoning capabilities. MATH-500 retention (83.7%) is slightly lower than GSM8K\n988\t    because MATH problems require deeper reasoning chains and more diverse mathematical\n989\t    operations, taxing the compressed model''s capacity more. The mean retention (87.5%)\n990\t    across 4 benchmarks closely matches GSM8K-only (86.7%), confirming that RASP recovery\n991\t    is not overfitting to the GSM8K recovery training distribution—the preserved reasoning\n992\t    scaffolding supports general reasoning ability, not just arithmetic. This addresses\n993\t    the \"GSM8K overfitting\" reviewer concern directly.\n994\t\n995\t    '\n996\t  tables:\n997\t  - name: 'Table: Cross-Benchmark Evaluation'\n998\t    columns:\n999\t    - Benchmark\n1000\t    - Baseline (%)\n1001\t    - RASP Recovered (%)\n1002\t    - Retention (%)\n1003\t    rows:\n1004\t    - - GSM8K\n1005\t      - 83.2\n1006\t      - 72.1\n1007\t      - 86.7\n1008\t    - - MATH-500\n1009\t      - 68.2\n1010\t      - 57.1\n1011\t      - 83.7\n1012\t    - - MMLU\n1013\t      - 63.1\n1014\t      - 58.4\n1015\t      - 92.6\n1016\t    - - HumanEval\n1017\t      - 52.4\n1018\t      - 45.1\n1019\t      - 86.1\n1020\t    - - Mean\n1021\t      - —\n1022\t      - —\n1023\t      - 87.5\n1024\t  figures_description:\n1025\t  - 'Radar chart: baseline vs RASP recovered across 4 benchmarks. Shows proportional\n1026\t    preservation across all capabilities.'\n1027\t  analysis: 'Cross-benchmark generalization is critical for EMNLP—it elevates the\n1028\t    paper from a GSM8K-specific technique to a general reasoning compression method.\n1029\t    The consistent ~87% retention across diverse benchmarks confirms that RASP preserves\n1030\t    the model''s reasoning scaffolding generally, not just arithmetic reasoning chains.\n1031\t\n1032\t    '\n1033\t- name: Bonsai Baseline at 20% Sparsity\n1034\t  plan_id: plan_013\n1035\t  description: 'Bonsai (ICML 2025) gradient-free structured pruning at 20% sparsity.\n1036\t    Lower priority baseline providing additional context for importance-only methods.\n1037\t\n1038\t    '\n1039\t  expected_results:\n1040\t    bonsai_post_prune_gsm8k: 0.052\n1041\t    bonsai_post_prune_ci95:\n1042\t    - 0.037\n1043\t    - 0.071\n1044\t    bonsai_post_sft_gsm8k: 0.158\n1045\t    bonsai_sft_grpo_gsm8k: 0.167\n1046\t    bonsai_recovery_rate: 0.201\n1047\t  result_rationale: 'Bonsai at 5.2% post-prune sits between Standard (1.4%) and GRASPrune\n1048\t    (17.8%), closer to Standard. Bonsai uses activation-based importance without gradients,\n1049\t    which provides slightly better structure selection than gradient-Taylor (Standard)\n1050\t    for reasoning models—activation magnitude captures some functional contribution\n1051\t    that gradient norms miss. However, without CKA or learned gates, Bonsai still\n1052\t    removes functionally unique structures. Recovery to 16.7% is minimal, confirming\n1053\t    that gradient-free importance is not the missing ingredient— CKA awareness is.\n1054\t    Bonsai is included primarily to round out the baseline comparison table and show\n1055\t    that RASP''s advantage is not specific to the choice of importance-only baseline.\n1056\t\n1057\t    '\n1058\t  tables:\n1059\t  - name: 'Table: Extended Baselines at 20% Sparsity'\n1060\t    columns:\n1061\t    - Method\n1062\t    - Post-Prune (%)\n1063\t    - Recovered (%)\n1064\t    - Recovery (%)\n1065\t    rows:\n1066\t    - - RASP\n1067\t      - 28.9\n1068\t      - 72.1\n1069\t      - 86.7\n1070\t    - - GRASPrune\n1071\t      - 17.8\n1072\t      - 41.3\n1073\t      - 49.6\n1074\t    - - Bonsai\n1075\t      - 5.2\n1076\t      - 16.7\n1077\t      - 20.1\n1078\t    - - Standard\n1079\t      - 1.4\n1080\t      - 12.4\n1081\t      - 14.9\n1082\t  figures_description: []\n1083\t  analysis: 'Bonsai confirms the ordering: RASP >> GRASPrune >> Bonsai > Standard.\n1084\t    The gradient-free vs gradient distinction (Bonsai vs Standard) is minor compared\n1085\t    to the CKA vs no-CKA distinction (RASP vs all others).\n1086\t\n1087\t    '\n1088\t- name: CKA Reliability Analysis\n1089\t  plan_id: plan_014\n1090\t  description: 'Split-half consistency and calibration size sensitivity for CKA-based\n1091\t    scoring. Tests whether F(h) values are stable enough to serve as a reliable pruning\n1092\t    signal.\n1093\t\n1094\t    '\n1095\t  expected_results:\n1096\t    split_half_spearman_rho: 0.94\n1097\t    split_half_spearman_p: <0.0001\n1098\t    split_half_n_heads: 896\n1099\t    calibration_256_vs_512_rank_correlation: 0.91\n1100\t    calibration_512_vs_1024_rank_correlation: 0.97\n1101\t    calibration_256_gsm8k: 0.274\n1102\t    calibration_512_gsm8k: 0.289\n1103\t    calibration_1024_gsm8k: 0.293\n1104\t    calibration_256_vs_512_pruned_set_overlap: 0.87\n1105\t    calibration_512_vs_1024_pruned_set_overlap: 0.95\n1106\t  result_rationale: 'Split-half Spearman ρ=0.94 confirms that CKA overlap scores are\n1107\t    highly consistent across independent calibration halves. This is expected because\n1108\t    linear CKA is a kernel-based statistic that converges quickly with sample size—512\n1109\t    mean-pooled activations provide >400 effective data points per head pair (after\n1110\t    centering), well above the threshold for stable estimation. Calibration sensitivity\n1111\t    shows diminishing returns: 256→512 improves ranking correlation by 6pp (0.91→0.97),\n1112\t    but 512→1024 adds only 0pp at the second decimal. The 512 default is near-optimal.\n1113\t    Downstream impact mirrors this: 256 samples yield 27.4% GSM8K (vs 28.9% at 512,\n1114\t    vs 29.3% at 1024)—a 1.9pp range that''s smaller than the inter-seed variance (plan_006).\n1115\t    The 87% pruned-set overlap at 256 vs 512 indicates that calibration size affects\n1116\t    only borderline heads; the core pruning decisions are stable. This reliability\n1117\t    evidence preempts reviewer concerns about CKA being a noisy or calibration- sensitive\n1118\t    signal.\n1119\t\n1120\t    '\n1121\t  tables:\n1122\t  - name: 'Table: CKA Scoring Reliability'\n1123\t    columns:\n1124\t    - Analysis\n1125\t    - Metric\n1126\t    - Value\n1127\t    rows:\n1128\t    - - Split-half consistency\n1129\t      - Spearman ρ\n1130\t      - 0.94\n1131\t    - - Split-half consistency\n1132\t      - p-value\n1133\t      - <0.0001\n1134\t    - - Calibration 256 samples\n1135\t      - GSM8K (%)\n1136\t      - 27.4\n1137\t    - - Calibration 512 samples\n1138\t      - GSM8K (%)\n1139\t      - 28.9\n1140\t    - - Calibration 1024 samples\n1141\t      - GSM8K (%)\n1142\t      - 29.3\n1143\t    - - Rank stability (256 vs 512)\n1144\t      - Spearman ρ\n1145\t      - 0.91\n1146\t    - - Rank stability (512 vs 1024)\n1147\t      - Spearman ρ\n1148\t      - 0.97\n1149\t  figures_description:\n1150\t  - 'Left panel: scatter plot of F(h) from split-half A vs split-half B (ρ=0.94).\n1151\t    Right panel: calibration sensitivity curve showing GSM8K accuracy vs calibration\n1152\t    set size (256/512/1024), with plateau at 512.'\n1153\t  analysis: CKA reliability is a housekeeping experiment but essential for credibility.\n1154\t    ρ=0.94 and the 512-sample plateau establish that RASP's scoring is practically\n1155\t    deterministic—not dependent on calibration luck. This combines with plan_006 (seed\n1156\t    robustness) to fully address the \"is this reproducible?\" question. For the paper,\n1157\t    this belongs in the appendix with a one-line reference in the main text.\n1158\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d5a2ac1e-eb55-43b9-94d1-02900c2c612b","uuid":"ec528341-25e4-4c75-87c3-c7952a5d6b68","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:10:20.360Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_17/projects/recovery_aware_struct_prune/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T233950/best/imagined_experiments.yaml","content":"tier: best\nnarrative_guidance: 'Original Redundancy Paradox framing holds completely. Three claims\n  require no softening. Story: CKA-aware pruning preserves reasoning scaffolding →\n  RLVR recovery redistributes function across retained structures → 65-78% recovery\n  vs <18% for standard pruning. GRASPrune as intermediate baseline confirms CKA is\n  the missing ingredient. Mixed-effects R²>0.25 validates CKA overlap as a per-structure\n  recoverability predictor. RLVR vs SFT interaction supports \"RL Squeezes, SFT Expands\"\n  mechanism. Clean EMNLP main paper with all three claims as section-level contributions.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Full GSM8K Evaluation (Baseline + RASP_20 + Standard_20)\n  plan_id: plan_001\n  description: 'Full 1319-sample GSM8K test evaluation for three configs: unpruned\n    baseline, RASP at 20% sparsity, and Standard (importance-only) at 20% sparsity.\n    Replaces N=100 quick evals with publication-quality numbers and 95% CIs.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    baseline_gsm8k_accuracy: 0.832\n    baseline_gsm8k_ci95:\n    - 0.811\n    - 0.852\n    baseline_n_correct: 1097\n    rasp_20_gsm8k_accuracy: 0.289\n    rasp_20_gsm8k_ci95:\n    - 0.265\n    - 0.314\n    rasp_20_n_correct: 381\n    standard_20_gsm8k_accuracy: 0.014\n    standard_20_gsm8k_ci95:\n    - 0.007\n    - 0.024\n    standard_20_n_correct: 18\n    rasp_vs_standard_gap_pp: 27.5\n    rasp_vs_standard_mcnemar_p: <0.0001\n    rasp_retention_rate: 0.347\n    standard_retention_rate: 0.017\n  result_rationale: 'Baseline settles at ~83% on full GSM8K (quick eval''s 94/100\n    was upward-biased by small N and favorable sample draw; 1319-sample eval regresses\n    toward the known DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B population accuracy of ~82-84% on\n    GSM8K). RASP_20 at 28.9% is consistent with quick eval''s 29/100, confirming that\n    CKA-aware structure selection preserves enough reasoning scaffolding for ~35%\n    retention. The key mechanism: RASP retains functionally unique heads (low F(h))\n    that encode irreplaceable reasoning steps—removing high-overlap heads leaves structures\n    whose function can potentially be redistributed during recovery. Standard_20 at\n    1.4% confirms importance-only pruning collapses reasoning: it preferentially retains\n    high-overlap \"safe\" structures while removing functionally unique ones, destroying\n    the reasoning scaffold entirely. The 27.5pp gap widens slightly from quick eval''s\n    28pp due to full-set evaluation capturing more multi-step problems where reasoning\n    chain integrity matters. McNemar''s test is trivially significant at N=1319.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 1: Post-Prune GSM8K Accuracy (1319 samples)'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - Sparsity\n    - GSM8K Acc (%)\n    - 95% CI\n    - Retention (%)\n    rows:\n    - - Baseline\n      - 0%\n      - 83.2\n      - '[81.1, 85.2]'\n      - —\n    - - RASP\n      - 20%\n      - 28.9\n      - '[26.5, 31.4]'\n      - 34.7\n    - - Standard\n      - 20%\n      - 1.4\n      - '[0.7, 2.4]'\n      - 1.7\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Bar chart with 95% CIs showing the three conditions. Y-axis: GSM8K accuracy (%).\n    Visual emphasis on the 27.5pp gap between RASP and Standard.'\n  analysis: 'Full-set evaluation tightens CIs from ±9pp (N=100) to ±2.4pp, establishing\n    statistical robustness. The 27.5pp gap is not marginal—it''s qualitative: RASP\n    preserves partial reasoning capability (35% retention) while Standard is near-chance.\n    This gap is the foundation for Claim 1: it determines whether recovery is possible\n    at all. The baseline at 83.2% also establishes the ceiling for recovery experiments.\n\n    '\n- name: SFT Warmup Recovery (RASP_20 + Standard_20)\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: 'Supervised fine-tuning on GSM8K train gold solutions (teacher-forcing)\n    to restore basic generation ability in pruned models. Fixes the no-EOS degeneration\n    problem observed in direct GRPO attempts. Both models trained in parallel with\n    identical hyperparameters and data budget.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    rasp_20_post_sft_gsm8k: 0.482\n    rasp_20_post_sft_ci95:\n    - 0.455\n    - 0.509\n    standard_20_post_sft_gsm8k: 0.147\n    standard_20_post_sft_ci95:\n    - 0.128\n    - 0.168\n    rasp_20_sft_gain_pp: 19.3\n    standard_20_sft_gain_pp: 13.3\n    rasp_20_eos_rate: 0.97\n    standard_20_eos_rate: 0.93\n    rasp_20_avg_response_length_tokens: 285\n    standard_20_avg_response_length_tokens: 342\n    sft_training_loss_rasp_final: 0.82\n    sft_training_loss_standard_final: 1.41\n    sft_epochs: 3\n    sft_learning_rate: 2.0e-05\n    sft_data_size: 7473\n  result_rationale: 'SFT warmup serves two functions: (1) fix generation pathology\n    (no-EOS infinite loops) by re-learning output format from gold solutions, and\n    (2) provide a structured reasoning template for subsequent GRPO optimization.\n    RASP_20 reaches ~48% because its preserved reasoning scaffolding (functionally\n    unique heads) can absorb gold-solution supervision—SFT \"expands\" correct paths\n    by imitation, and RASP''s intact reasoning structures provide the substrate for\n    this expansion. Standard_20 reaches only ~15% because SFT can teach output format\n    but cannot reconstruct the destroyed reasoning scaffold—it''s learning surface\n    patterns without the underlying multi-step inference machinery. The 33pp gap after\n    SFT (48% vs 15%) is wider than post-prune (29% vs 1%) in absolute terms because\n    SFT amplifies the structural advantage: models with intact scaffolding benefit\n    more from supervised reasoning demonstrations. Training loss confirms this—RASP\n    converges to 0.82 (learning the reasoning patterns) while Standard plateaus at\n    1.41 (unable to fit multi-step solutions without the structural capacity). Both\n    models achieve >93% EOS rate, confirming SFT fixes generation pathology regardless\n    of pruning method.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 2: Post-SFT GSM8K Accuracy'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - Post-Prune (%)\n    - Post-SFT (%)\n    - SFT Gain (pp)\n    - EOS Rate (%)\n    rows:\n    - - RASP_20\n      - 28.9\n      - 48.2\n      - 19.3\n      - 97\n    - - Standard_20\n      - 1.4\n      - 14.7\n      - 13.3\n      - 93\n  figures_description:\n  - Training loss curves (RASP vs Standard) over SFT steps, showing RASP's faster\n    convergence and lower final loss. Include EOS rate subplot showing both models\n    recovering proper termination.\n  analysis: 'SFT-only results (48.2% vs 14.7%) already demonstrate RASP''s advantage,\n    but the critical observation is that SFT alone cannot close the gap to baseline—RASP\n    reaches 58% of baseline (48.2/83.2) while Standard reaches only 18%. This motivates\n    GRPO: SFT provides the generation scaffold, GRPO optimizes reasoning paths. The\n    \"SFT Expands\" mechanism is visible here—RASP''s preserved unique structures absorb\n    supervision efficiently, while Standard''s destroyed scaffold limits SFT''s ceiling.\n\n    '\n- name: GRPO Recovery on SFT-Warmed Models\n  plan_id: plan_003\n  description: 'Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) recovery on SFT-warmed RASP_20\n    and Standard_20 models. Core MVP-completion experiment demonstrating that RASP\n    enables RLVR recovery while Standard cannot meaningfully recover. Both models\n    trained with identical GRPO hyperparameters (max_completion_length=4096 for R1-style\n    long CoT).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    rasp_20_sft_grpo_gsm8k: 0.721\n    rasp_20_sft_grpo_ci95:\n    - 0.697\n    - 0.744\n    standard_20_sft_grpo_gsm8k: 0.124\n    standard_20_sft_grpo_ci95:\n    - 0.107\n    - 0.143\n    rasp_recovery_rate: 0.867\n    standard_recovery_rate: 0.149\n    rasp_grpo_gain_over_sft_pp: 23.9\n    standard_grpo_gain_over_sft_pp: -2.3\n    rasp_grpo_reward_mean_final: 0.68\n    standard_grpo_reward_mean_final: 0.11\n    rasp_grpo_reward_std_final: 0.22\n    standard_grpo_reward_std_final: 0.08\n    rasp_grpo_kl_divergence_final: 3.2\n    standard_grpo_kl_divergence_final: 0.9\n    grpo_training_steps: 2000\n    grpo_group_size: 8\n    grpo_learning_rate: 5.0e-07\n    grpo_max_completion_length: 4096\n  result_rationale: 'GRPO recovery is where the Redundancy Paradox manifests most\n    clearly. RASP_20 jumps from 48.2% (post-SFT) to 72.1%—a 23.9pp gain—because GRPO''s\n    exploration-based optimization (\"RL Squeezes\") can discover and reinforce alternative\n    reasoning paths through the retained structures. The key mechanism: RASP preserved\n    functionally unique heads (low CKA overlap), which serve as irreplaceable anchors\n    for reasoning chains. GRPO redistributes the function of pruned high-overlap heads\n    across these anchors, effectively compressing the reasoning computation. The 72.1%\n    represents 86.7% of baseline (72.1/83.2), consistent with the \"near-baseline recovery\"\n    thesis. Standard_20 shows no GRPO benefit (14.7→12.4%, actually declining 2.3pp)\n    because the destroyed reasoning scaffold provides no structural substrate for\n    GRPO to optimize—there are no functionally unique reasoning anchors to redistribute\n    through. GRPO''s exploration finds no rewarding paths because the model lacks\n    the representational capacity for multi-step reasoning. The slight decline reflects\n    GRPO''s KL penalty destabilizing the fragile SFT-learned patterns without discovering\n    better alternatives. The reward signal confirms this: RASP achieves mean reward\n    0.68 (finding correct solutions), while Standard plateaus at 0.11 (near-random).\n    RASP''s higher KL (3.2 vs 0.9) indicates genuine policy movement—the model is\n    exploring and finding better reasoning paths. Standard''s low KL means it barely\n    moves from SFT initialization because exploration is unrewarding.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 3: SFT→GRPO Recovery Pipeline Results'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - Post-Prune (%)\n    - Post-SFT (%)\n    - Post-GRPO (%)\n    - Recovery Rate (%)\n    rows:\n    - - RASP_20\n      - 28.9\n      - 48.2\n      - 72.1\n      - 86.7\n    - - Standard_20\n      - 1.4\n      - 14.7\n      - 12.4\n      - 14.9\n    - - Baseline\n      - 83.2\n      - —\n      - —\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - GRPO reward curves over training steps for RASP_20 vs Standard_20. RASP shows\n    steady climb to 0.68; Standard flatlines near 0.11. Include KL divergence subplot.\n  - Waterfall chart showing accuracy at each pipeline stage (post-prune → post-SFT\n    → post-GRPO) for both methods. Visual narrative of RASP's progressive recovery\n    vs Standard's ceiling.\n  analysis: 'This is the headline result. RASP enables 86.7% recovery while Standard\n    achieves only 14.9%—a qualitative difference between \"recoverable\" and \"unrecoverable.\"\n    The GRPO stage contributes +23.9pp for RASP but -2.3pp for Standard, demonstrating\n    that RLVR recovery requires structural prerequisites that only CKA-aware pruning\n    preserves. The \"RL Squeezes\" mechanism is confirmed: GRPO compresses reasoning\n    through retained unique structures, which requires those structures to exist.\n    This supports Claim 1 definitively and motivates Claims 2-3.\n\n    '\n- name: Full GSM8K Eval on All Recovered Models\n  plan_id: plan_004\n  description: 'Publication-quality 1319-sample GSM8K evaluation on all recovered\n    models from plan_003. Final numbers for paper Table 1 with bootstrapped CIs.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    rasp_20_recovered_gsm8k: 0.721\n    rasp_20_recovered_ci95_bootstrap:\n    - 0.694\n    - 0.746\n    standard_20_recovered_gsm8k: 0.124\n    standard_20_recovered_ci95_bootstrap:\n    - 0.104\n    - 0.146\n    rasp_vs_standard_recovered_gap_pp: 59.7\n    effect_size_cohens_h: 1.28\n    rasp_recovery_fraction_of_baseline: 0.867\n    standard_recovery_fraction_of_baseline: 0.149\n    n_samples: 1319\n    bootstrap_iterations: 10000\n  result_rationale: 'Full-set evaluation with bootstrap CIs confirms the GRPO results\n    are not artifacts of evaluation noise. The 59.7pp gap between recovered models\n    (72.1% vs 12.4%) is even larger than the post-prune gap (27.5pp), demonstrating\n    that the recovery pipeline amplifies the structural advantage. Cohen''s h of 1.28\n    is a \"very large\" effect size, ruling out any marginal-improvement narrative.\n    Bootstrap CIs (10K iterations) provide non-parametric confidence bounds suitable\n    for publication. These numbers populate the paper''s main results table and abstract.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 1 (Paper): Main Results at 20% Structured Sparsity'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - Post-Prune (%)\n    - Post-SFT (%)\n    - Post-SFT+GRPO (%)\n    - Recovery (%)\n    - 95% CI\n    rows:\n    - - Baseline\n      - '83.2'\n      - —\n      - —\n      - —\n      - '[81.1, 85.2]'\n    - - RASP\n      - '28.9'\n      - '48.2'\n      - '72.1'\n      - '86.7'\n      - '[69.4, 74.6]'\n    - - Standard\n      - '1.4'\n      - '14.7'\n      - '12.4'\n      - '14.9'\n      - '[10.4, 14.6]'\n    - - GRASPrune\n      - 17.8†\n      - 32.6†\n      - 41.3†\n      - 49.6†\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Grouped bar chart: post-prune vs post-recovery accuracy for all methods. Arrows\n    showing recovery magnitude. This is Figure 1 of the paper.'\n  analysis: 'Table 1 is the paper''s anchor result. The narrative is clear: same model,\n    same sparsity, same recovery budget—only the pruning criterion differs, and it\n    determines whether recovery succeeds (87%) or fails (15%). The GRASPrune row (plan_005,\n    marked with †) will be added once that experiment completes.\n\n    '\n- name: GRASPrune Baseline at 20% Sparsity\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: 'Full pipeline for GRASPrune (ACL 2026 SOTA budgeted pruning) at 20%\n    sparsity: gate training → pruning → eval → SFT warmup → GRPO recovery. Primary\n    external baseline to demonstrate that importance-aware pruning without CKA is\n    insufficient.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    graspune_post_prune_gsm8k: 0.178\n    graspune_post_prune_ci95:\n    - 0.157\n    - 0.2\n    graspune_post_sft_gsm8k: 0.326\n    graspune_post_sft_grpo_gsm8k: 0.413\n    graspune_post_sft_grpo_ci95:\n    - 0.386\n    - 0.441\n    graspune_recovery_rate: 0.496\n    graspune_gate_training_epochs: 5\n    graspune_gate_loss_final: 0.34\n    graspune_vs_rasp_post_prune_gap_pp: -11.1\n    graspune_vs_rasp_recovered_gap_pp: -30.8\n  result_rationale: 'GRASPrune at 17.8% post-prune sits between RASP (28.9%) and Standard\n    (1.4%), confirming it as the right intermediate baseline. GRASPrune''s learned\n    gates provide better importance estimation than static metrics (explaining its\n    advantage over Standard), but without CKA-awareness it still removes some functionally\n    unique reasoning heads. Post-prune gap to RASP is 11.1pp— substantial but not\n    as dramatic as the 27.5pp RASP-Standard gap. The critical observation emerges\n    in recovery: GRASPrune recovers to only 41.3% (49.6% of baseline) vs RASP''s 72.1%\n    (86.7%). The recovery gap (30.8pp) is nearly 3× the post-prune gap (11.1pp), demonstrating\n    that recovery amplifies structural choices—CKA-unaware pruning destroys some irreplaceable\n    scaffolding that GRPO cannot recover. GRASPrune''s gate training does capture\n    some functional redundancy implicitly (through gradient flow), explaining its\n    partial recoverability, but the explicit CKA signal in RASP provides superior\n    structure selection. This confirms the paper''s core argument: CKA overlap is\n    the \"missing ingredient\" that separates good importance estimation from recovery-optimal\n    pruning.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: GRASPrune Pipeline Results'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - Post-Prune (%)\n    - Post-SFT (%)\n    - Post-GRPO (%)\n    - Recovery (%)\n    rows:\n    - - RASP\n      - 28.9\n      - 48.2\n      - 72.1\n      - 86.7\n    - - GRASPrune\n      - 17.8\n      - 32.6\n      - 41.3\n      - 49.6\n    - - Standard\n      - 1.4\n      - 14.7\n      - 12.4\n      - 14.9\n  figures_description:\n  - Three-method comparison bar chart at each pipeline stage. GRASPrune clearly intermediate,\n    confirming the ablation narrative.\n  - 'Scatter plot: per-head CKA overlap (x) vs heads retained by each method (color).\n    Shows RASP retains low-F(h) heads that GRASPrune partially removes.'\n  analysis: 'GRASPrune as intermediate baseline is essential for the paper''s argument.\n    It breaks the potential confound that RASP''s advantage is simply better importance\n    estimation—GRASPrune has arguably better importance estimation (learned gates\n    vs static Taylor) but worse recovery. The 30.8pp recovery gap vs only 11.1pp post-prune\n    gap quantifies the \"CKA premium\" in recovery-aware pruning. Reviewers asking \"is\n    this just a better pruning metric?\" are answered directly: no, CKA captures something\n    orthogonal to importance that specifically enables RLVR recovery.\n\n    '\n- name: Multi-Seed RASP_20 Post-Prune Evaluation\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: 'RASP pruning and evaluation at 20% sparsity with seeds 123 and 7 (original\n    was seed 42). Tests robustness of the 29% post-prune result across different CKA\n    calibration samples and pruning tie-breaking.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    seed_42_gsm8k: 0.289\n    seed_123_gsm8k: 0.281\n    seed_7_gsm8k: 0.302\n    mean_across_seeds: 0.291\n    std_across_seeds: 0.011\n    cv_across_seeds: 0.037\n    max_min_range_pp: 2.1\n    seed_123_overlap_with_42_pruned_set: 0.91\n    seed_7_overlap_with_42_pruned_set: 0.89\n  result_rationale: 'RASP''s scoring is deterministic given fixed calibration data—seed\n    variation affects only the GSM8K calibration sample selection (512 of 7473 train\n    samples). Since CKA overlap is computed over 512 mean-pooled activations and averaged\n    across all intra-layer pairs, the statistic is stable: different calibration subsets\n    sample from the same activation distribution. Seed 123 yields 28.1% (slightly\n    lower due to a calibration set with fewer multi-step problems, marginally shifting\n    one borderline head''s F(h) across the pruning threshold in layer 14). Seed 7\n    yields 30.2% (calibration set slightly enriched in diverse problem types, producing\n    sharper CKA discrimination). Standard deviation of 1.1pp across 3 seeds is well\n    within the [0, 3]pp best-tier bound, confirming that the 29% result is not seed-dependent.\n    The 89-91% overlap in pruned head sets across seeds shows that RASP''s structure\n    selection is highly stable—the same ~179 heads are pruned regardless of calibration\n    seed, with only 16-20 heads varying at the margin. This rules out the \"lucky seed\"\n    concern.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Multi-Seed Robustness'\n    columns:\n    - Seed\n    - GSM8K (%)\n    - Pruned Set Overlap w/ Seed 42 (%)\n    rows:\n    - - 42\n      - 28.9\n      - —\n    - - 123\n      - 28.1\n      - 91\n    - - 7\n      - 30.2\n      - 89\n    - - Mean ± Std\n      - 29.1 ± 1.1\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - Strip plot with three seed points and mean±std band. Demonstrates low variance\n    and confirms robustness.\n  analysis: 'σ=1.1pp across seeds establishes that RASP''s structural advantage is\n    robust, not a calibration artifact. The high overlap in pruned sets (89-91%) shows\n    the ranking is stable. This is a necessary control for Claim 1—without it, reviewers\n    could dismiss the 29% result as seed-specific. Combined with full-set evaluation\n    (plan_001), this provides both precision and robustness.\n\n    '\n- name: Scoring Ablation at 20% Sparsity\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: 'Controlled ablation of the RASP scoring function. Four conditions\n    at 20% sparsity: magnitude-only (L2 norm), gradient-only (Taylor expansion = I(h)),\n    random pruning, and CKA-only (F(h) without importance weighting). Isolates the\n    contribution of each scoring component.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    magnitude_only_gsm8k: 0.038\n    gradient_only_gsm8k: 0.014\n    random_pruning_gsm8k: 0.067\n    cka_only_gsm8k: 0.213\n    rasp_full_gsm8k: 0.289\n    standard_importance_gsm8k: 0.014\n    magnitude_only_ci95:\n    - 0.023\n    - 0.058\n    gradient_only_ci95:\n    - 0.007\n    - 0.024\n    random_pruning_ci95:\n    - 0.049\n    - 0.089\n    cka_only_ci95:\n    - 0.191\n    - 0.237\n  result_rationale: 'This ablation reveals a surprising finding: CKA-only scoring\n    (21.3%) vastly outperforms all importance-only variants (1.4-3.8%), and even random\n    pruning (6.7%) beats importance-based methods. The explanation crystallizes the\n    Redundancy Paradox. Importance-only methods (magnitude=3.8%, gradient=1.4%) remove\n    \"unimportant\" structures that happen to be functionally unique— these structures\n    carry specialized reasoning functions not reflected in simple importance metrics.\n    By contrast, CKA-only scoring protects unique structures regardless of measured\n    importance, preserving the reasoning scaffold. Random pruning at 6.7% outperforms\n    importance-based methods because random selection has no bias toward removing\n    unique structures— it removes high-overlap and low-overlap structures equiprobably.\n    Gradient-only (1.4%) matches Standard because Standard IS gradient-based Taylor\n    importance (I(h)). Magnitude-only (3.8%) does slightly better because L2 norm\n    captures structural capacity more broadly. The full RASP score (28.9%) outperforms\n    CKA-only (21.3%) by 7.6pp because importance weighting correctly identifies which\n    unique structures are also functionally important—pure CKA protection without\n    importance wastes budget retaining unique-but-useless structures. This confirms\n    both components contribute to the RASP score, but CKA is the dominant factor (21.3%\n    vs 1.4-3.8% for importance variants), supporting Claim 2.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Scoring Ablation at 20% Sparsity'\n    columns:\n    - Scoring Method\n    - Formula\n    - GSM8K (%)\n    - 95% CI\n    rows:\n    - - RASP (full)\n      - I(h) × (1-F(h))^α\n      - 28.9\n      - '[26.5, 31.4]'\n    - - CKA-only\n      - (1-F(h))^α\n      - 21.3\n      - '[19.1, 23.7]'\n    - - Random\n      - Uniform random\n      - 6.7\n      - '[4.9, 8.9]'\n    - - Magnitude-only\n      - '||w_h||₂'\n      - 3.8\n      - '[2.3, 5.8]'\n    - - Gradient-only\n      - '||w_h ⊙ ∇w_h||₂'\n      - 1.4\n      - '[0.7, 2.4]'\n    - - Standard (= Gradient)\n      - I(h) ascending\n      - 1.4\n      - '[0.7, 2.4]'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Horizontal bar chart ranked by accuracy. Clear visual hierarchy: RASP > CKA-only\n    >> Random >> Magnitude > Gradient = Standard. Annotate the ''Redundancy Paradox''\n    region where importance methods underperform random.'\n  analysis: 'The ablation provides direct evidence for the Redundancy Paradox: importance-\n    based pruning is worse than random for reasoning model compression. This is because\n    importance metrics are anti-correlated with functional uniqueness in reasoning\n    LLMs—\"important\" heads tend to be high-overlap (redundant) heads that contribute\n    to many reasoning paths, while \"unimportant\" heads may be specialized for specific\n    reasoning operations. CKA''s dominance (21.3% vs 3.8%) confirms that functional\n    overlap is the primary signal for reasoning preservation. The RASP premium over\n    CKA-only (+7.6pp) shows importance still contributes when combined with CKA.\n\n    '\n- name: Per-Structure CKA-Recoverability Correlation\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: 'Mixed-effects regression analysis at the per-head level. For each\n    of 896 attention heads, extract: CKA overlap F(h), importance I(h), weight norm,\n    gradient magnitude, activation magnitude, and post-recovery activation change\n    (recoverability proxy). Layer as random effect.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_heads_total: 896\n    n_layers: 32\n    mixed_effects_r2_marginal: 0.37\n    mixed_effects_r2_conditional: 0.58\n    cka_overlap_beta: 0.42\n    cka_overlap_se: 0.06\n    cka_overlap_p: <0.0001\n    weight_norm_beta: 0.08\n    weight_norm_p: 0.12\n    gradient_importance_beta: 0.11\n    gradient_importance_p: 0.034\n    activation_magnitude_beta: 0.05\n    activation_magnitude_p: 0.31\n    cka_partial_r2: 0.22\n    importance_partial_r2: 0.04\n    layer_random_effect_variance: 0.15\n    icc: 0.21\n    vif_max: 2.8\n    durbin_watson: 1.89\n    breusch_pagan_p: 0.18\n  result_rationale: 'Mixed-effects regression with marginal R²=0.37 validates CKA\n    overlap as a meaningful predictor of recoverability, exceeding the [0.25, 0.45]\n    best-tier bound comfortably. The key finding: CKA overlap''s standardized β=0.42\n    (p<0.0001) dominates all other predictors—weight norm (β=0.08, n.s.), gradient\n    importance (β=0.11, marginal), and activation magnitude (β=0.05, n.s.) are comparatively\n    weak. Partial R² for CKA alone is 0.22, meaning CKA uniquely explains 22% of recoverability\n    variance beyond what other metrics capture. This is because CKA overlap measures\n    functional redundancy— the degree to which a head''s computation is replicated\n    elsewhere in the layer—which directly predicts whether GRPO can redistribute that\n    head''s function. Importance metrics measure current contribution but not redistributability.\n    The conditional R²=0.58 (vs marginal 0.37) shows layer identity explains additional\n    variance—early layers (0-5) have lower recoverability per unit of CKA overlap\n    (their functions are more position- specific), while middle layers (10-20) show\n    strongest CKA-recoverability coupling (most reasoning redistribution occurs here).\n    ICC=0.21 confirms non-trivial layer clustering, justifying the mixed-effects specification.\n    Diagnostics are clean: VIF<3 (no multicollinearity), Durbin-Watson≈2 (no autocorrelation),\n    Breusch-Pagan n.s. (homoscedastic residuals).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Mixed-Effects Regression on Per-Head Recoverability'\n    columns:\n    - Predictor\n    - β (std)\n    - SE\n    - t\n    - p\n    - Partial R²\n    rows:\n    - - F(h) (CKA overlap)\n      - 0.42\n      - 0.06\n      - 7.0\n      - <0.0001\n      - 0.22\n    - - I(h) (gradient importance)\n      - 0.11\n      - 0.05\n      - 2.13\n      - 0.034\n      - 0.04\n    - - '||w_h||₂ (weight norm)'\n      - 0.08\n      - 0.05\n      - 1.55\n      - 0.12\n      - 0.01\n    - - Act. magnitude\n      - 0.05\n      - 0.05\n      - 1.02\n      - 0.31\n      - <0.01\n    - - —\n      - —\n      - —\n      - —\n      - —\n      - —\n    - - Marginal R²\n      - 0.37\n      - —\n      - —\n      - —\n      - —\n    - - Conditional R²\n      - 0.58\n      - —\n      - —\n      - —\n      - —\n    - - ICC (layer)\n      - 0.21\n      - —\n      - —\n      - —\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Scatter plot: per-head F(h) (x-axis) vs recoverability (y-axis), colored by layer\n    depth. Marginal regression line with 95% prediction band. Shows positive relationship\n    with layer-dependent intercept variation.'\n  - Coefficient plot with 95% CIs for all predictors. CKA overlap clearly separated\n    from zero; other predictors near zero or marginal.\n  - Layer-specific random effect estimates (caterpillar plot). Shows middle layers\n    have highest intercepts, early layers lowest.\n  analysis: 'This is the core evidence for Claim 2. CKA overlap (β=0.42) is 4× stronger\n    than the next-best predictor (gradient importance, β=0.11) and uniquely explains\n    22% of recoverability variance. The marginal R²=0.37 means CKA + standard metrics\n    explain 37% of recovery outcomes—substantial for a single- model per-head analysis.\n    The layer random effects (ICC=0.21) reveal mechanistic structure: reasoning redistribution\n    is concentrated in middle layers (10-20), consistent with the \"RL Squeezes\" mechanism\n    operating on the reasoning computation core. This validates RASP''s design: using\n    F(h) to guide pruning decisions captures a signal about post-recovery behavior\n    that standard importance metrics miss.\n\n    '\n- name: SFT-Only Recovery Comparison\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: 'Same pruned models (RASP_20, Standard_20), same data budget, but SFT-only\n    recovery (no GRPO). Tests Claim 3: whether recovery method changes optimal pruning\n    order. Combined with plan_003 results, enables 2×2 interaction analysis.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    sft_only_rasp_gsm8k: 0.482\n    sft_only_standard_gsm8k: 0.147\n    sft_only_rasp_ci95:\n    - 0.455\n    - 0.509\n    sft_only_standard_ci95:\n    - 0.128\n    - 0.168\n    sft_grpo_rasp_gsm8k: 0.721\n    sft_grpo_standard_gsm8k: 0.124\n    grpo_marginal_gain_rasp_pp: 23.9\n    grpo_marginal_gain_standard_pp: -2.3\n    interaction_term_p: 0.0023\n    interaction_effect_size_pp: 26.2\n    rasp_advantage_under_sft_pp: 33.5\n    rasp_advantage_under_grpo_pp: 59.7\n    rasp_rank_sft: 1\n    rasp_rank_grpo: 1\n    standard_sft_recovery_rate: 0.177\n    standard_grpo_recovery_rate: 0.149\n  result_rationale: 'SFT-only recovery reveals the pruning×method interaction central\n    to Claim 3. Under SFT-only recovery, RASP''s advantage is 33.5pp (48.2% vs 14.7%)—\n    significant but not catastrophic for Standard. Under SFT+GRPO, RASP''s advantage\n    explodes to 59.7pp (72.1% vs 12.4%). The interaction is driven by GRPO''s asymmetric\n    effect: +23.9pp for RASP but -2.3pp for Standard. The mechanism is \"RL Squeezes,\n    SFT Expands\": SFT recovery works by expanding correct solution paths through imitation—it\n    benefits from any preserved structure and doesn''t strongly depend on functional\n    redundancy patterns. Both RASP and Standard benefit from SFT proportionally to\n    their preserved capacity. GRPO recovery works by compressing incorrect paths and\n    reinforcing correct ones through exploration—it requires functional redundancy\n    (overlapping structures whose function can be redistributed) to find alternative\n    reasoning routes. RASP preserves this redundancy by design; Standard destroys\n    it. The interaction p=0.0023 is highly significant, and the 26.2pp interaction\n    effect size confirms that optimal pruning strategy genuinely depends on the intended\n    recovery method. Importantly, Standard under SFT-only (14.7%) slightly outperforms\n    Standard under GRPO (12.4%), suggesting that GRPO can actively harm models without\n    the structural prerequisites for RL-based recovery—the KL penalty destabilizes\n    learned patterns without discovering better alternatives.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Pruning × Recovery Method Interaction (2×2)'\n    columns:\n    - Pruning\n    - SFT-Only (%)\n    - SFT+GRPO (%)\n    - GRPO Marginal (pp)\n    rows:\n    - - RASP\n      - 48.2\n      - 72.1\n      - 23.9\n    - - Standard\n      - 14.7\n      - 12.4\n      - -2.3\n    - - RASP Advantage (pp)\n      - 33.5\n      - 59.7\n      - —\n  - name: 'Table: Interaction Test'\n    columns:\n    - Effect\n    - Estimate (pp)\n    - SE\n    - p\n    rows:\n    - - Pruning (RASP vs Std)\n      - 46.6\n      - 3.2\n      - <0.0001\n    - - Method (GRPO vs SFT)\n      - 10.8\n      - 3.1\n      - '0.0006'\n    - - Pruning × Method\n      - 26.2\n      - 4.5\n      - '0.0023'\n  figures_description:\n  - '2×2 interaction plot: x-axis = recovery method (SFT, SFT+GRPO), y-axis = GSM8K\n    accuracy, separate lines for RASP and Standard. Non-parallel lines demonstrate\n    the interaction. This is Figure 3 of the paper.'\n  - 'Marginal GRPO gain comparison: paired bar chart showing +23.9pp (RASP) vs -2.3pp\n    (Standard). Visual emphasis on the divergent GRPO effect.'\n  analysis: 'The interaction effect (p=0.0023, 26.2pp) directly supports Claim 3.\n    The story is compelling: SFT recovery is method-agnostic (benefits from any preserved\n    structure), while GRPO recovery is structure-dependent (requires the specific\n    redundancy pattern that RASP preserves). This means the optimal pruning strategy\n    depends on the intended recovery method—a novel finding not predicted by existing\n    pruning or recovery literature. The negative GRPO marginal for Standard (-2.3pp)\n    strengthens the narrative: GRPO doesn''t just fail to help Standard-pruned models,\n    it actively hurts them.\n\n    '\n- name: 25% Sparsity RASP + Standard\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: 'Full pipeline (prune → eval → SFT → GRPO → eval) at 25% sparsity for\n    both RASP and Standard. Tests robustness across sparsity levels and whether RASP''s\n    advantage narrows or widens at higher compression.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    rasp_25_post_prune_gsm8k: 0.184\n    rasp_25_post_prune_ci95:\n    - 0.164\n    - 0.206\n    standard_25_post_prune_gsm8k: 0.003\n    standard_25_post_prune_ci95:\n    - 0.001\n    - 0.01\n    rasp_25_post_sft_gsm8k: 0.356\n    standard_25_post_sft_gsm8k: 0.068\n    rasp_25_sft_grpo_gsm8k: 0.581\n    rasp_25_sft_grpo_ci95:\n    - 0.554\n    - 0.607\n    standard_25_sft_grpo_gsm8k: 0.054\n    standard_25_sft_grpo_ci95:\n    - 0.04\n    - 0.07\n    rasp_25_recovery_rate: 0.698\n    standard_25_recovery_rate: 0.065\n    rasp_25_vs_20_post_prune_delta_pp: -10.5\n    rasp_25_vs_20_recovered_delta_pp: -14.0\n  result_rationale: 'At 25% sparsity, RASP''s post-prune accuracy drops to 18.4% (from\n    28.9% at 20%), losing ~10.5pp from the additional 5% structural removal. Standard\n    drops to 0.3% (from 1.4%), essentially zero. The gap widens slightly in absolute\n    terms (18.1pp vs 27.5pp at 20%) but the critical question is recoverability. RASP_25\n    recovers to 58.1% (69.8% of baseline)—lower than RASP_20''s 72.1% but still demonstrating\n    substantial recovery. The ~14pp drop from 20% to 25% reflects the removal of additional\n    functionally unique structures that, while RASP tried to protect, had to be pruned\n    to meet the sparsity target. Each additional unique head removed reduces the recovery\n    ceiling because fewer anchors exist for GRPO''s redistribution mechanism. Standard_25\n    at 5.4% post- recovery shows that additional pruning further destroys recovery\n    potential. The 52.7pp gap at 25% (58.1% vs 5.4%) is comparable to the 59.7pp gap\n    at 20% (72.1% vs 12.4%), confirming RASP''s advantage is robust across sparsity\n    levels. The diminishing returns from 20→25% suggest a natural operating point:\n    20% maximizes recovery quality for this model.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Sparsity Scaling (RASP vs Standard)'\n    columns:\n    - Sparsity\n    - Method\n    - Post-Prune (%)\n    - Recovered (%)\n    - Recovery Rate (%)\n    rows:\n    - - 20%\n      - RASP\n      - 28.9\n      - 72.1\n      - 86.7\n    - - 20%\n      - Standard\n      - 1.4\n      - 12.4\n      - 14.9\n    - - 25%\n      - RASP\n      - 18.4\n      - 58.1\n      - 69.8\n    - - 25%\n      - Standard\n      - 0.3\n      - 5.4\n      - 6.5\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Line plot: sparsity (x-axis, 20% and 25%) vs recovered accuracy (y-axis), separate\n    lines for RASP and Standard. Shows RASP''s graceful degradation vs Standard''s\n    collapse. Extrapolation dashes toward 30%.'\n  analysis: 'The 25% sparsity results confirm that RASP''s advantage is not an artifact\n    of the specific 20% operating point. RASP maintains meaningful recoverability\n    (69.8%) even at higher compression, while Standard''s already-minimal recovery\n    degrades further. This multi-sparsity evidence strengthens the generalizability\n    claim and provides practical guidance: RASP enables aggressive compression with\n    recovery, while Standard collapses even at moderate sparsity.\n\n    '\n- name: 30% Sparsity Probe\n  plan_id: plan_011\n  description: 'Prune + eval only (no recovery) for RASP and Standard at 30% sparsity.\n    Maps the threshold where both methods collapse, contextualizing the operating\n    range of the Redundancy Paradox.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    rasp_30_post_prune_gsm8k: 0.062\n    rasp_30_post_prune_ci95:\n    - 0.045\n    - 0.083\n    standard_30_post_prune_gsm8k: 0.001\n    standard_30_post_prune_ci95:\n    - 0.0\n    - 0.006\n    rasp_30_n_unique_heads_remaining: 14\n    standard_30_n_unique_heads_remaining: 3\n  result_rationale: 'At 30% sparsity, RASP post-prune drops to 6.2%—still above zero\n    but approaching the collapse zone observed at 35% (0% for both methods). This\n    is consistent with the D005 finding: the DeepSeek-R1-Distill model, having been\n    distilled from a larger model, has less structural redundancy than a natively-trained\n    model of equivalent size. At 30%, even RASP must remove some functionally unique\n    heads, leaving too few reasoning anchors for meaningful computation. Standard\n    at 0.1% is effectively zero. The 30% probe establishes the upper bound of RASP''s\n    operating range for this model and provides context for the 20-25% results: RASP''s\n    advantage is most pronounced in the 20-25% range where it can still protect critical\n    scaffolding. Beyond 30%, both methods fail because the model simply doesn''t have\n    enough structural redundancy to support reasoning at that compression. We do not\n    attempt recovery at 30% because the post-prune accuracy is too low (6.2%) to provide\n    meaningful signal—plan_003 showed that GRPO requires a minimum functional baseline\n    to discover rewarding paths.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Sparsity Scaling (Probe)'\n    columns:\n    - Sparsity\n    - Method\n    - Post-Prune (%)\n    - Status\n    rows:\n    - - 20%\n      - RASP\n      - 28.9\n      - Recoverable\n    - - 25%\n      - RASP\n      - 18.4\n      - Recoverable\n    - - 30%\n      - RASP\n      - 6.2\n      - Near-collapse\n    - - 35%\n      - RASP\n      - 0.0\n      - Collapsed (D005)\n    - - 20%\n      - Standard\n      - 1.4\n      - Collapsed\n    - - 25%\n      - Standard\n      - 0.3\n      - Collapsed\n    - - 30%\n      - Standard\n      - 0.1\n      - Collapsed\n    - - 35%\n      - Standard\n      - 0.0\n      - Collapsed (D005)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Sparsity curve: x-axis sparsity (20-35%), y-axis post-prune GSM8K, lines for\n    RASP and Standard. RASP shows graceful degradation with knee at ~28%; Standard\n    is flat near zero. Shaded ''recoverable zone'' for RASP between 20-25%.'\n  analysis: 'The sparsity curve reveals RASP''s operating envelope: meaningful reasoning\n    preservation up to ~25%, rapid degradation at 30%, collapse at 35%. Standard collapses\n    immediately at 20%. This positions RASP as enabling a ~25% \"recoverable sparsity\"\n    window that doesn''t exist for importance-only pruning. The 35% collapse for both\n    methods contextualizes the model''s inherent redundancy budget—distilled models\n    have less slack than natively trained ones.\n\n    '\n- name: Extended Evaluation on Best Recovered Config\n  plan_id: plan_012\n  description: 'Evaluate the best recovered model (RASP_20 SFT+GRPO) on MMLU (5-shot),\n    HumanEval (pass@1), and MATH-500 to test generalization beyond GSM8K.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    baseline_mmlu: 0.631\n    rasp_recovered_mmlu: 0.584\n    rasp_mmlu_retention: 0.926\n    baseline_humaneval: 0.524\n    rasp_recovered_humaneval: 0.451\n    rasp_humaneval_retention: 0.861\n    baseline_math500: 0.682\n    rasp_recovered_math500: 0.571\n    rasp_math500_retention: 0.837\n    mean_retention_across_benchmarks: 0.875\n    gsm8k_retention_for_reference: 0.867\n  result_rationale: 'RASP recovery generalizes beyond GSM8K. MMLU retention (92.6%)\n    is highest because MMLU primarily tests knowledge retrieval and pattern matching,\n    which are less affected by structural pruning than multi-step reasoning—the retained\n    structures preserve factual knowledge effectively. HumanEval retention (86.1%)\n    is comparable to GSM8K (86.7%) because code generation requires similar multi-step\n    reasoning capabilities. MATH-500 retention (83.7%) is slightly lower than GSM8K\n    because MATH problems require deeper reasoning chains and more diverse mathematical\n    operations, taxing the compressed model''s capacity more. The mean retention (87.5%)\n    across 4 benchmarks closely matches GSM8K-only (86.7%), confirming that RASP recovery\n    is not overfitting to the GSM8K recovery training distribution—the preserved reasoning\n    scaffolding supports general reasoning ability, not just arithmetic. This addresses\n    the \"GSM8K overfitting\" reviewer concern directly.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Cross-Benchmark Evaluation'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - Baseline (%)\n    - RASP Recovered (%)\n    - Retention (%)\n    rows:\n    - - GSM8K\n      - 83.2\n      - 72.1\n      - 86.7\n    - - MATH-500\n      - 68.2\n      - 57.1\n      - 83.7\n    - - MMLU\n      - 63.1\n      - 58.4\n      - 92.6\n    - - HumanEval\n      - 52.4\n      - 45.1\n      - 86.1\n    - - Mean\n      - —\n      - —\n      - 87.5\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Radar chart: baseline vs RASP recovered across 4 benchmarks. Shows proportional\n    preservation across all capabilities.'\n  analysis: 'Cross-benchmark generalization is critical for EMNLP—it elevates the\n    paper from a GSM8K-specific technique to a general reasoning compression method.\n    The consistent ~87% retention across diverse benchmarks confirms that RASP preserves\n    the model''s reasoning scaffolding generally, not just arithmetic reasoning chains.\n\n    '\n- name: Bonsai Baseline at 20% Sparsity\n  plan_id: plan_013\n  description: 'Bonsai (ICML 2025) gradient-free structured pruning at 20% sparsity.\n    Lower priority baseline providing additional context for importance-only methods.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    bonsai_post_prune_gsm8k: 0.052\n    bonsai_post_prune_ci95:\n    - 0.037\n    - 0.071\n    bonsai_post_sft_gsm8k: 0.158\n    bonsai_sft_grpo_gsm8k: 0.167\n    bonsai_recovery_rate: 0.201\n  result_rationale: 'Bonsai at 5.2% post-prune sits between Standard (1.4%) and GRASPrune\n    (17.8%), closer to Standard. Bonsai uses activation-based importance without gradients,\n    which provides slightly better structure selection than gradient-Taylor (Standard)\n    for reasoning models—activation magnitude captures some functional contribution\n    that gradient norms miss. However, without CKA or learned gates, Bonsai still\n    removes functionally unique structures. Recovery to 16.7% is minimal, confirming\n    that gradient-free importance is not the missing ingredient— CKA awareness is.\n    Bonsai is included primarily to round out the baseline comparison table and show\n    that RASP''s advantage is not specific to the choice of importance-only baseline.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Extended Baselines at 20% Sparsity'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - Post-Prune (%)\n    - Recovered (%)\n    - Recovery (%)\n    rows:\n    - - RASP\n      - 28.9\n      - 72.1\n      - 86.7\n    - - GRASPrune\n      - 17.8\n      - 41.3\n      - 49.6\n    - - Bonsai\n      - 5.2\n      - 16.7\n      - 20.1\n    - - Standard\n      - 1.4\n      - 12.4\n      - 14.9\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: 'Bonsai confirms the ordering: RASP >> GRASPrune >> Bonsai > Standard.\n    The gradient-free vs gradient distinction (Bonsai vs Standard) is minor compared\n    to the CKA vs no-CKA distinction (RASP vs all others).\n\n    '\n- name: CKA Reliability Analysis\n  plan_id: plan_014\n  description: 'Split-half consistency and calibration size sensitivity for CKA-based\n    scoring. Tests whether F(h) values are stable enough to serve as a reliable pruning\n    signal.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    split_half_spearman_rho: 0.94\n    split_half_spearman_p: <0.0001\n    split_half_n_heads: 896\n    calibration_256_vs_512_rank_correlation: 0.91\n    calibration_512_vs_1024_rank_correlation: 0.97\n    calibration_256_gsm8k: 0.274\n    calibration_512_gsm8k: 0.289\n    calibration_1024_gsm8k: 0.293\n    calibration_256_vs_512_pruned_set_overlap: 0.87\n    calibration_512_vs_1024_pruned_set_overlap: 0.95\n  result_rationale: 'Split-half Spearman ρ=0.94 confirms that CKA overlap scores are\n    highly consistent across independent calibration halves. This is expected because\n    linear CKA is a kernel-based statistic that converges quickly with sample size—512\n    mean-pooled activations provide >400 effective data points per head pair (after\n    centering), well above the threshold for stable estimation. Calibration sensitivity\n    shows diminishing returns: 256→512 improves ranking correlation by 6pp (0.91→0.97),\n    but 512→1024 adds only 0pp at the second decimal. The 512 default is near-optimal.\n    Downstream impact mirrors this: 256 samples yield 27.4% GSM8K (vs 28.9% at 512,\n    vs 29.3% at 1024)—a 1.9pp range that''s smaller than the inter-seed variance (plan_006).\n    The 87% pruned-set overlap at 256 vs 512 indicates that calibration size affects\n    only borderline heads; the core pruning decisions are stable. This reliability\n    evidence preempts reviewer concerns about CKA being a noisy or calibration- sensitive\n    signal.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: CKA Scoring Reliability'\n    columns:\n    - Analysis\n    - Metric\n    - Value\n    rows:\n    - - Split-half consistency\n      - Spearman ρ\n      - 0.94\n    - - Split-half consistency\n      - p-value\n      - <0.0001\n    - - Calibration 256 samples\n      - GSM8K (%)\n      - 27.4\n    - - Calibration 512 samples\n      - GSM8K (%)\n      - 28.9\n    - - Calibration 1024 samples\n      - GSM8K (%)\n      - 29.3\n    - - Rank stability (256 vs 512)\n      - Spearman ρ\n      - 0.91\n    - - Rank stability (512 vs 1024)\n      - Spearman ρ\n      - 0.97\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Left panel: scatter plot of F(h) from split-half A vs split-half B (ρ=0.94).\n    Right panel: calibration sensitivity curve showing GSM8K accuracy vs calibration\n    set size (256/512/1024), with plateau at 512.'\n  analysis: CKA reliability is a housekeeping experiment but essential for credibility.\n    ρ=0.94 and the 512-sample plateau establish that RASP's scoring is practically\n    deterministic—not dependent on calibration luck. This combines with plan_006 (seed\n    robustness) to fully address the \"is this reproducible?\" question. For the paper,\n    this belongs in the appendix with a one-line reference in the main text.\n","numLines":1158,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1158}}}
