{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FQiJB1yYHeg2cPmw1mdmcC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tmeta:\n2\t  venue: EMNLP 2026\n3\t  track: main\n4\t  template: \"\"\n5\t  template_note: \"ARR May 25 submission. ACL template downloaded and set up in docs/paper/. All sections written fresh at 20% sparsity.\"\n6\t  page_limit: 8\n7\t  deadline: 2026-05-25\n8\t  core_claim: \"The optimal pruning strategy depends on the recovery paradigm: CKA-based functional overlap predicts RLVR recoverability, creating a Redundancy Paradox where structures traditionally 'safe' to prune (high overlap) are precisely those most recoverable under RLVR.\"\n9\t  created_at: 2026-05-15T12:00:00Z\n10\t  updated_at: 2026-05-15T18:00:00Z\n11\t  existing_drafts:\n12\t    - paper/introduction.tex\n13\t    - paper/method.tex\n14\t    - paper/related_work.tex\n15\t    - paper/experiments.tex\n16\t  draft_issues:\n17\t    - \"[RESOLVED] All .tex rewritten from ACL template at 20% sparsity\"\n18\t    - \"experiments.tex has many [TBD] placeholders (awaiting GRPO results)\"\n19\t    - \"[RESOLVED] main.tex, references.bib, abstract.tex, conclusion.tex created\"\n20\t    - \"[RESOLVED] Introduction contribution list — remove 'most comprehensive study' (single model+benchmark)\"\n21\t  reviewer_corrections:\n22\t    - id: RC-001\n23\t      date: 2026-05-15\n24\t      description: \"α ablation values standardized to {0.0, 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0} (5 points). α=4.0 dropped. α=0.25 retained for low-CKA-weight interval.\"\n25\t    - id: RC-002\n26\t      date: 2026-05-15\n27\t      description: \"Delete 'most comprehensive study to date' — single model + single benchmark does not support.\"\n28\t    - id: RC-003\n29\t      date: 2026-05-15\n30\t      description: \"Fig 2 (accuracy comparison) and Fig 4 (recovery trajectory) blocked by GRPO v2 results. Do NOT pre-generate.\"\n31\t\n32\tnarrative_constraints:\n33\t  updated_at: 2026-05-15T18:00:00Z\n34\t  source: \"Director review of narrative robustness\"\n35\t\n36\t  evidence_restrictions:\n37\t    - id: ER-001\n38\t      description: \"SFT N=100 greedy eval (57% RASP vs 64% Standard) CANNOT be cited as Claim 3 evidence\"\n39\t      reason: \"CI overlap, difference not statistically significant at N=100\"\n40\t      scope: \"Claim 3 only — these numbers may appear in Table 1 as descriptive data, but must not support the ordering-reversal claim\"\n41\t    - id: ER-002\n42\t      description: \"Post-prune 29% vs 1% (28pp gap, p<0.0001) remains valid evidence for Claim 1\"\n43\t      scope: \"Claim 1, Introduction hook, Abstract\"\n44\t\n45\t  dual_version_framework:\n46\t    description: \"Prepare two narrative versions; selection depends on GRPO Stage 2 results\"\n47\t    decision_trigger: \"grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v2 and grpo_stage2_standard_20_v2 completion\"\n48\t\n49\t    strong_version:\n50\t      condition: \"GRPO confirms RASP >> Standard (statistically significant gap)\"\n51\t      claims: [C1, C2, C3]\n52\t      narrative: \"Full Redundancy Paradox + ordering reversal. Claim 3 uses GRPO data as primary evidence for recovery-method-conditioned pruning ordering.\"\n53\t      tab2_role: \"Primary evidence table for Claim 3 — ranking reversal between SFT and GRPO recovery\"\n54\t\n55\t    weak_version:\n56\t      condition: \"GRPO ambiguous (RASP ≈ Standard, no significant gap)\"\n57\t      claims: [C1, C2]\n58\t      claim3_status: \"Downgraded to exploratory observation, not a formal contribution\"\n59\t      narrative: \"Focus on post-prune evidence (29% vs 1%) + pruning-recovery interaction analysis (Claim 2 mixed-effects regression). Claim 3 presented as 'preliminary observation consistent with RL Squeezes hypothesis' without claiming confirmation.\"\n60\t      tab2_role: \"Descriptive table showing recovery stages, not evidence for ordering reversal\"\n61\t      introduction_adjustment: \"Contribution item 3 reframed as 'preliminary evidence suggesting recovery-method dependence' rather than 'recovery method conditions optimal pruning order'\"\n62\t\n63\t  introduction_constraints:\n64\t    - \"Headline result uses post-prune 29% vs 1% (Claim 1 evidence, always valid)\"\n65\t    - \"NO GRPO-specific numbers in Introduction — use placeholder [TBD] or qualitative language ('RLVR recovery further widens/maintains this gap')\"\n66\t    - \"Contribution list C3 phrased conditionally until GRPO results decide version\"\n67\t\n68\t  method_constraints:\n69\t    - \"Method section writing unaffected by dual-version — proceed normally\"\n70\t    - \"Stage 3 (recovery) description is factual procedure, not claim-dependent\"\n71\t\n72\tpaper_outline:\n73\t  - section: abstract\n74\t    word_budget: 200\n75\t    core_bullets:\n76\t      - \"Problem: Structured pruning and post-pruning recovery are treated independently; no work studies how pruning decisions affect RLVR recoverability\"\n77\t      - \"Insight: Redundancy Paradox — functional overlap (CKA) predicts RLVR recoverability; high-overlap structures are easily redistributable\"\n78\t      - \"Method: RASP scores S(h) = I(h) × (1-F(h))^α, protecting functionally unique reasoning scaffolding\"\n79\t      - \"Result: At 20% sparsity on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, RASP preserves 29% vs 1% post-prune GSM8K; RLVR recovers RASP to [TBD]% while Standard reaches [TBD]%\"\n80\t    dependencies: [experiments, method]\n81\t    tables: []\n82\t    figures: []\n83\t    registry_refs: [mvp_rasp_pipeline_v4, grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v2, grpo_stage2_standard_20_v2]\n84\t\n85\t  - section: introduction\n86\t    word_budget: 1200\n87\t    core_bullets:\n88\t      - \"Hook: At 20% structured sparsity, standard importance-based pruning collapses reasoning to 1% GSM8K while RASP preserves 29% — same parameter budget, qualitatively different outcome\"\n89\t      - \"Gap: Pruning methods optimize for immediate accuracy; recovery methods take whatever architecture they receive. No work studies whether what you prune determines whether RLVR can recover\"\n90\t      - \"Insight: Redundancy Paradox — traditional pruning safely removes 'redundant' (high-overlap) structures, but these are precisely the most RLVR-recoverable via global exploration and path compression\"\n91\t      - \"Mechanism: RASP integrates intra-layer CKA functional overlap into pruning scoring, extends LTH to paradigm-conditioned prunability\"\n92\t      - \"C1: CKA-aware pruning preserves reasoning (29% vs 1%, enables vs prevents recovery)\"\n93\t      - \"C2: Per-structure F(h) predicts recoverability (mixed-effects regression)\"\n94\t      - \"C3: [DUAL-VERSION] Strong: Recovery method conditions optimal pruning order (GRPO reverses SFT ranking). Weak: Preliminary evidence suggests recovery paradigm interacts with pruning structure selection\"\n95\t    dependencies: [experiments]\n96\t    citation_hints:\n97\t      - \"kornblith2019cka, rlsqueezes2026, selfhealing2025\"\n98\t      - \"graspprune2026, resp2025, bonsai2025\"\n99\t      - \"mpruner2024, frankle2019lth\"\n100\t    figures: [fig1_rasp_pipeline]\n101\t    tables: []\n102\t    registry_refs: [mvp_rasp_pipeline_v4, sft_rasp_20, sft_standard_20]\n103\t    constraints:\n104\t      - \"Headline result = post-prune 29% vs 1% ONLY. No GRPO numbers.\"\n105\t      - \"See narrative_constraints.introduction_constraints\"\n106\t      - \"RC-002: Delete 'most comprehensive study to date'. Single model+benchmark does not support this claim.\"\n107\t\n108\t  - section: related_work\n109\t    word_budget: 800\n110\t    core_bullets:\n111\t      - \"Cluster 1 — Structured Pruning for LLMs: LLM-Pruner→Bonsai→GRASPrune→RESP; width vs depth (ShortGPT/LLMs2LRMs); none optimizes for post-recovery performance\"\n112\t      - \"Cluster 2 — Pruning-Aware Recovery: Self-Healing (RLVR recovery, single ratio), P²Law (scaling), PASER (data selection), PAT (joint prune-tune); all treat pruning as black box\"\n113\t      - \"Cluster 3 — RLVR Training Dynamics: RL Squeezes SFT Expands → theoretical basis; OPSD → post-RL compression\"\n114\t      - \"Cluster 4 — CKA-based Methods: MPruner/Lachance (inter-layer depth pruning) vs RASP (intra-layer width pruning for recoverability)\"\n115\t      - \"Cluster 5 — Function-Aware Pruning: FANG (activation grouping), FuncNet (ICA networks); similar motivation but no recovery component\"\n116\t    dependencies: [introduction]\n117\t    tables: []\n118\t    figures: []\n119\t    registry_refs: []\n120\t\n121\t  - section: method\n122\t    word_budget: 1500\n123\t    core_bullets:\n124\t      - \"Preliminaries: structured pruning notation, GQA constraints (G=7, 4 KV groups/layer), RLVR/GRPO background\"\n125\t      - \"Stage 1 — Reasoning-Trace Structural Analysis: self-calibrated CoT traces (512 GSM8K); importance I(h) via Taylor expansion (Eq 1); CKA functional overlap F(h) via intra-layer pairwise CKA (Eq 2-3)\"\n126\t      - \"Stage 2 — Recovery-Aware Scoring: S(h) = I(h) × (1-F(h))^α (Eq 4); three regimes (irreplaceable/redundant-important/unimportant); per-layer capacity floor (≥40% MLP per layer)\"\n127\t      - \"Stage 3 — Two-Stage Recovery: SFT warmup (restore EOS/format) → GRPO RLVR (restore reasoning); justified by EOS collapse in direct GRPO\"\n128\t      - \"Algorithm 1 pseudocode\"\n129\t    dependencies: []\n130\t    notation_table:\n131\t      \"\\\\\\\\mathcal{M}\": \"Pretrained transformer model\"\n132\t      \"\\\\\\\\mathcal{H}\": \"Set of prunable structures\"\n133\t      \"I(h)\": \"Structural importance (Taylor expansion)\"\n134\t      \"F(h)\": \"Functional overlap (intra-layer CKA)\"\n135\t      \"S(h)\": \"Recovery-aware score\"\n136\t      \"\\\\\\\\alpha\": \"Overlap penalty weight (default 1.0)\"\n137\t      \"G\": \"GQA group size (7 for Qwen2.5-7B)\"\n138\t      \"\\\\\\\\mathcal{G}_\\\\\\\\ell\": \"GQA groups in layer ell\"\n139\t    figures: [fig1_rasp_pipeline]\n140\t    tables: []\n141\t    registry_refs: []\n142\t\n143\t  - section: experiments\n144\t    word_budget: 2000\n145\t    core_bullets:\n146\t      - \"Setup: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B (28 layers, GQA 28Q/4KV), GSM8K cal=512 / eval=1319, 20% structured sparsity\"\n147\t      - \"Baselines: Standard (importance-only), GRASPrune [TBD], Random\"\n148\t      - \"Main results Table 1: pruning method × recovery stage {post-prune, +SFT, +SFT+GRPO}\"\n149\t      - \"Claim 3 — Table 2: [DUAL-VERSION] Strong: GRPO reverses SFT ranking (RASP>Standard under GRPO, Standard>RASP under SFT-only). Weak: Table 2 as descriptive recovery trajectory, Claim 3 downgraded to observation. NOTE: SFT 57% vs 64% (N=100) CANNOT be used as ordering-reversal evidence (ER-001: CI overlap, not significant). Post-prune 29% vs 1% remains valid.\"\n150\t      - \"Scoring ablation Table 3: I-only vs F-only vs RASP (S=I×(1-F)^α) [TODO: plan_015]\"\n151\t      - \"Per-structure analysis: CKA overlap F(h) vs recoverability correlation (mixed-effects regression) [TODO: plan_008]\"\n152\t      - \"[TBD: α ablation plan_015, inference speedup plan_016, unstructured baseline plan_017]\"\n153\t    dependencies: []\n154\t    registry_refs:\n155\t      - mvp_rasp_pipeline_v4\n156\t      - sft_rasp_20\n157\t      - sft_standard_20\n158\t      - grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v2\n159\t      - grpo_stage2_standard_20_v2\n160\t    tables: [tab1_main_results, tab2_recovery_comparison, tab3_scoring_ablation, tab4_hyperparams]\n161\t    figures: [fig2_accuracy_comparison, fig3_cka_heatmap, fig4_recovery_trajectory]\n162\t\n163\t  - section: conclusion\n164\t    word_budget: 400\n165\t    core_bullets:\n166\t      - \"Summary: RASP exploits Redundancy Paradox — functional overlap signals recoverability, not just redundancy\"\n167\t      - \"Limitations: single model (DeepSeek-R1-7B), single benchmark (GSM8K), single sparsity (20%), α not auto-tuned\"\n168\t      - \"Future: multi-model validation, integration with GRASPrune backbone, α auto-tuning via validation, extension to semi-structured pruning\"\n169\t    dependencies: [experiments, method]\n170\t    tables: []\n171\t    figures: []\n172\t    registry_refs: []\n173\t\n174\tvisualization_plan:\n175\t  - fig_id: fig1_rasp_pipeline\n176\t    type: conceptual\n177\t    tool: paperbanana\n178\t    description: \"RASP 3-stage pipeline overview. Left: Stage 1 (reasoning traces → I(h) importance + F(h) CKA overlap). Center: Stage 2 (S(h) scoring with Redundancy Paradox intuition — high-overlap heads marked for pruning). Right: Stage 3 (SFT warmup → GRPO RLVR recovery). Show contrast with standard pruning flow.\"\n179\t    placement: introduction\n180\t    aspect_ratio: \"16:9\"\n181\t\n182\t  - fig_id: fig2_accuracy_comparison\n183\t    type: statistical\n184\t    tool: matplotlib\n185\t    description: \"Grouped bar chart: GSM8K accuracy (%) across recovery stages. X-axis: {Unpruned, Post-Prune, +SFT, +SFT+GRPO}. Groups: RASP (blue) vs Standard (red). Annotate 29% vs 1% gap at post-prune. GRPO bars [TBD].\"\n186\t    data_source: \"registry: mvp_rasp_pipeline_v4, sft_rasp_20, sft_standard_20, grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v2, grpo_stage2_standard_20_v2\"\n187\t    data_available: \"BLOCKED — GRPO v2 experiments RUNNING. Do NOT pre-generate (RC-003).\"\n188\t    placement: experiments\n189\t    aspect_ratio: \"4:3\"\n190\t\n191\t  - fig_id: fig3_cka_heatmap\n192\t    type: statistical\n193\t    tool: matplotlib\n194\t    description: \"CKA functional overlap heatmap: 28 layers × 4 GQA groups. Color intensity = F(h). Overlay: mark pruned groups for RASP (×) vs Standard (○) to visualize different pruning decisions.\"\n195\t    data_source: \"registry: mvp_rasp_pipeline_v4\"\n196\t    placement: experiments\n197\t    aspect_ratio: \"3:4\"\n198\t\n199\t  - fig_id: fig4_recovery_trajectory\n200\t    type: statistical\n201\t    tool: matplotlib\n202\t    description: \"GRPO training dynamics: reward (or accuracy) curves over training steps for RASP-pruned vs Standard-pruned models. Show divergence in recoverability.\"\n203\t    data_source: \"registry: grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v2, grpo_stage2_standard_20_v2\"\n204\t    data_available: \"BLOCKED — GRPO v2 experiments RUNNING. Do NOT pre-generate (RC-003).\"\n205\t    placement: experiments\n206\t    aspect_ratio: \"4:3\"\n207\t\n208\ttable_plan:\n209\t  - table_id: tab1_main_results\n210\t    caption: \"Main results at 20% structured sparsity on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B (GSM8K accuracy %).\"\n211\t    placement: experiments\n212\t    type: comparison\n213\t    columns: [Method, Post-Prune, \"+SFT\", \"+SFT+GRPO\"]\n214\t    data_source: \"registry: mvp_rasp_pipeline_v4, sft_rasp_20, sft_standard_20, grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v2, grpo_stage2_standard_20_v2\"\n215\t    notes: \"Rows: Unpruned (94%), Standard, RASP, GRASPrune [TBD]. Bold best per column. SFT column shows descriptive data only (N=100 greedy); do NOT use SFT 57% vs 64% gap as evidence for ordering reversal (ER-001).\"\n216\t\n217\t  - table_id: tab2_recovery_comparison\n218\t    caption: \"Recovery method interaction: SFT vs GRPO recovery on differently-pruned models.\"\n219\t    placement: experiments\n220\t    type: comparison\n221\t    columns: [Pruning, Post-Prune, \"+SFT\", \"+GRPO-direct\", \"+SFT+GRPO\"]\n222\t    data_source: \"registry: sft_rasp_20, sft_standard_20, grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v2, grpo_stage2_standard_20_v2\"\n223\t    notes: \"[DUAL-VERSION] Strong: Supports Claim 3 ordering reversal via GRPO column. Weak: Descriptive recovery trajectory only, no ordering-reversal claim. GRPO-direct column shows direct GRPO failure (both methods). ER-001: SFT ranking (64% > 57%) not statistically significant, cannot support ordering reversal.\"\n224\t\n225\t  - table_id: tab3_scoring_ablation\n226\t    caption: \"Scoring component ablation at 20% sparsity.\"\n227\t    placement: experiments\n228\t    type: ablation\n229\t    columns: [Scoring, Post-Prune, \"+SFT+GRPO\"]\n230\t    data_source: \"manual\"\n231\t    baseline_row: \"RASP S(h)=I(h)×(1-F(h))^α\"\n232\t    alpha_values: [0.0, 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0]\n233\t    notes: \"[TODO: requires plan_015 α ablation. RC-001: 5 α values {0.0, 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0}. α=4.0 dropped, α=0.25 retained.]\"\n234\t\n235\t  - table_id: tab4_hyperparams\n236\t    caption: \"Training configuration for SFT warmup and GRPO recovery stages.\"\n237\t    placement: experiments\n238\t    type: configuration\n239\t    columns: [Parameter, SFT, GRPO]\n240\t    data_source: \"registry: sft_rasp_20, grpo_stage2_rasp_20_v2\"\n241\t\n242\tliterature_strategy:\n243\t  citation_budget: 35\n244\t  macro_context:\n245\t    - \"structured pruning for reasoning LLMs\"\n246\t    - \"RLVR training dynamics and post-pruning recovery\"\n247\t    - \"representation similarity measures (CKA) for model analysis\"\n248\t  micro_clusters:\n249\t    - cluster_name: \"Structured Pruning\"\n250\t      search_queries:\n251\t        - \"structured pruning large language models 2025 2026\"\n252\t        - \"attention head pruning MLP channel pruning reasoning\"\n253\t      must_cite:\n254\t        - \"LLM-Pruner\"\n255\t        - \"GRASPrune (ACL 2026)\"\n256\t        - \"RESP (arXiv Dec 2025)\"\n257\t        - \"Bonsai (ICML 2025)\"\n258\t      target_count: 10\n259\t\n260\t    - cluster_name: \"Pruning Recovery\"\n261\t      search_queries:\n262\t        - \"pruning recovery RLVR reinforcement learning reasoning model\"\n263\t        - \"post-pruning fine-tuning recovery scaling\"\n264\t      must_cite:\n265\t        - \"Self-Healing (2025)\"\n266\t        - \"P² Law (ACL 2025)\"\n267\t        - \"PASER\"\n268\t        - \"PAT (AAAI 2025)\"\n269\t      target_count: 6\n270\t\n271\t    - cluster_name: \"RLVR Dynamics\"\n272\t      search_queries:\n273\t        - \"RLVR training dynamics reinforcement learning reasoning 2026\"\n274\t        - \"GRPO group relative policy optimization\"\n275\t      must_cite:\n276\t        - \"RL Squeezes SFT Expands (ICLR 2026)\"\n277\t        - \"GRPO (Shao et al.)\"\n278\t        - \"DeepSeek-R1\"\n279\t      target_count: 5\n280\t\n281\t    - c